{"parentUuid":"76f01024-69b1-42bb-89a0-a3c32d4527ea","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"aeede644703c0347a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffmujzEfkxwJL5nsMsec","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WHTaumQ2s1mCmDgdSkdcTt","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/docs/ATOMIZE_BRIEF.md","description":"Read atomize brief"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":774,"cache_read_input_tokens":55713,"output_tokens":150,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":38},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":774},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":150,"cache_read_input_tokens":55713,"cache_creation_input_tokens":774,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":774,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01WHTaumQ2s1mCmDgdSkdcTt":{"command":"cat /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/docs/ATOMIZE_BRIEF.md","description":"Read atomize brief"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffmujjbpQk4LYWWUjHNG","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"10bd9400-1e23-4d9a-869f-db3a253f7cb8","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:26:57.165Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"10bd9400-1e23-4d9a-869f-db3a253f7cb8","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"18f95e19-3d7d-4312-a698-c0ed8ed1d357","agentId":"aeede644703c0347a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WHTaumQ2s1mCmDgdSkdcTt","type":"tool_result","content":"# Atomization brief (shared by every Flint ingest agent)\n\nRead this whole file before writing anything. It holds the rules the Harries review taught (2026-09-26: 25 of 35 files needed fixes). The review gate will check every rule here.\n\n## Schema\n\nFollow `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\.claude\\skills\\ingest-book\\SKILL.md` exactly: `_domain.md`, `_book.md` (map and resume checkpoint, updated after EACH chapter or unit), and entries named `<kind>--<kebab-slug>.md` with kind one of principle, framework, case-study, heuristic, claim. Use the frontmatter keys exactly as the skill gives them. The body has **What it is** / **Why it works** / **When to apply** / **When NOT to apply** (mandatory, non-trivial) / **Example** / **Related**.\n\nStyle reference, read before writing: `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\SecondBrain\\Knowledge\\library\\strength-and-conditioning\\fundamentals-of-sprinting--moyer\\case-study--isometric-sprinter-and-hurdler.md`, plus one principle file in the same folder.\n\n## Voices: label every one\n\n- **The source author's statements are plain text.**\n- **Eric's views** (from `docs/method-baseline.md` or his reviews) are prefixed \"Eric's baseline:\" or \"Eric's review:\".\n- **Your own reasoning, application or synthesis** is prefixed \"Inference:\".\n- **Nothing unlabeled that the author did not say.** The most common failures: a judgment from Eric's review written as if the author said it; a mechanism the author never gave presented as \"why it works\" (write \"The author gives no mechanism. Inference: ...\"); an invented example.\n\n## Lessons from the Supertraining Chapter 1 review (2026-09-26: 30 FIX, 1 NO-GO of 41)\n\n- **Keep the source's internal contradictions.** When a book says X on one page and not-X on another, record both with their pages and set `contested`. Never pick one side silently. The NO-GO entry reversed the book's SJ-CMJ strength-deficit rule because the book states it both ways.\n- **Name the right voice.** In co-authored books, write \"the authors\" unless a passage clearly belongs to one author. A passage that names Verkhoshansky in the third person is Siff writing.\n- **Keep `proven` rare and scoped.** Scope it to the replicated core (for example, \"Hill's force-velocity curve\"), never the whole entry, and mark any disputed sentence inside it. Four of eight `proven` ratings were lowered.\n- **No mechanism the source does not give.** About a dozen \"Why it works\" sections supplied physiology the book never states. Label it \"Inference:\" or leave it out.\n- **Do not add \"well established\" or \"modern research shows\" without a citation.** Name the paper, or delete the phrase.\n\n## Lessons from the 2026-09-27 review wave (Supertraining Ch2-3, Issurin Ch3, Bondarchuk, Evans, Old Bull)\n\n- **Invented Examples are the most common failure.** 34 of 36 Supertraining Ch2-3a Examples were unlabelled inventions. Use the source's own example where it gives one; otherwise start the Example with \"Inference:\".\n- **\"Verified against the page image\" means you opened the image.** Four Issurin Ch3 entries claimed it for figures they had misread (Fig 3.7 inverted, a Table 3.7 row swapped). Sparse slides are not \"bare diagrams\" until you have looked.\n- **Name the population and method the source gives.** Bondarchuk's data are 7,000+ native and foreign athletes, questionnaires, r >= 0.349, steroid-free; entries that said \"Soviet athletes\" were wrong everywhere.\n- **Check what a cited paper actually says.** Issurin 2016's critique targets Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model, not Issurin's A-T-R; Herzog & Leonard 2000 shows force enhancement, against the book's sentence. When the book misreports a study, record both.\n- **No review history in entries.** \"Prior version\", \"corrected from a NO-GO finding\" and similar belong in `staging/fixes_*_applied.md`, never in the entry body.\n- **Fix logs must match the files.** One fixer log claimed fixes that were not made. Fixers run their own grep checks and paste the output.\n- **Section headers use the vault form** `**What it is** —` (with the em dash). Do not add suffixes such as \": mandatory\". Em dashes are banned only in titles and body text. Never wrap a `[[wikilink]]` across lines.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Every number, definition and quote carries a checkable locator: PDF page (and printed page if it differs), or file plus timestamp. Never write \"confirmed in PDF\" unless you opened that page.\n- Never invent a number. Keep ambiguity visible: mark unclear audio or OCR as unclear, and do not harmonize two conflicting numbers from the source; record both with their locators.\n- OCR sources (`staging/ocr/*.md`): pages listed as low_confidence in the header must be checked against the page image before any number from them is cited. Use the Read tool on the PDF page, or render it.\n- **OCR confidence scores miss the worst errors** (checked 2026-09-27). Zatsiorsky 2e: the OCR interleaves the two columns and the sidebars line by line, so its text is not readable prose; use it only to find content, and read and quote from the page image. *CFTS*: single-column prose that mostly reads in order, but upside-down lines come out as gibberish, some lines are dropped, and figures are scrambled, all at \"0 low-confidence pages\". For any OCR source, quote only after checking the page image, and read every figure and table from the image.\n\n## Confidence\n\n- Practitioner courses and lectures: ceiling `supported`.\n- Books: `proven` only for established, widely replicated physiology or mechanics. Soviet-era or single-lab empirical findings and training prescriptions: `supported` at most. `untested` for single cases and anecdotes. `contested` where another library source or modern evidence disagrees (say which).\n\n## Dedup and converge\n\nBefore each entry, grep `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\SecondBrain\\Knowledge\\library` and the staging library (`C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage\\staging\\library`) for the concept. If it is covered, add a new entry only for a genuinely new angle, and link the existing one in Related. List reciprocal-link suggestions in your final report. Never edit files in the vault or another agent's staging folder.\n\n## Baylor context\n\nRead `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage\\docs\\method-baseline.md` (approved). Use it in When to apply, labelled \"Eric's baseline:\". Department names: Athletic Performance, Athletic Medicine, Applied Performance. Plain style, no em dashes. Use commas, colons and parentheses.\n\n## Scope and output\n\n- Write ONLY into your assigned staging folder. No git, no vault edits.\n- Stop at a chapter or unit boundary. It is better to finish fewer chapters well; the `_book.md` checkpoint lets the next batch resume.\n- Final reply: entries by kind and by confidence; chapters or units done and pending with page ranges; reciprocal-link suggestions; unclear or contradictory spots in the source; anything you could not read.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"b64c1442-7f70-49e9-b3e6-db87ac771f33","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:26:57.378Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"# Atomization brief (shared by every Flint ingest agent)\n\nRead this whole file before writing anything. It holds the rules the Harries review taught (2026-09-26: 25 of 35 files needed fixes). The review gate will check every rule here.\n\n## Schema\n\nFollow `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\.claude\\skills\\ingest-book\\SKILL.md` exactly: `_domain.md`, `_book.md` (map and resume checkpoint, updated after EACH chapter or unit), and entries named `<kind>--<kebab-slug>.md` with kind one of principle, framework, case-