{"parentUuid":"e7b40f73-a622-4508-baa6-f98d23e69376","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"aeede644703c0347a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffnBxfvGCaQj7tTNbndx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Quc5Jje1tHggWsV8QiPSV5","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"V=/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library; S=/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library; \necho \"### ISSURIN brief\"; cat \"$V/research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a.md\" | head -80","description":"Read the Issurin 2016 vault brief"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6948,"cache_read_input_tokens":170236,"output_tokens":347,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":137},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6948},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":347,"cache_read_input_tokens":170236,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6948,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6948,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01Quc5Jje1tHggWsV8QiPSV5":{"command":"V=/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library; S=/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library; \necho \"### ISSURIN brief\"; cat \"$V/research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a.md\" | head -80","description":"Read the Issurin 2016 vault brief"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffnBkG3qVFmJTJ1GZexC","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"9cc2b0a1-caff-40b8-bb94-c1fe8079d862","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:30:39.074Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"9cc2b0a1-caff-40b8-bb94-c1fe8079d862","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"9f0b5c05-18c3-490f-86c4-7f58fb0f08fd","agentId":"aeede644703c0347a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Quc5Jje1tHggWsV8QiPSV5","type":"tool_result","content":"### ISSURIN brief\n---\ntype: paper-brief\nstatus: ingested\ncreated: 2026-07-31\nupdated: 2026-07-31\nsource: zotero\nzotero-item-key: PT9QTQ2I\nzotero-attachment-key: 46JPR6ZS\nzotero-version: 5193\ncitekey: \"issurinBenefitsLimitationsBlock2016\"\ndoi: \"10.1007/s40279-015-0425-5\"\ndomain: [\"sport-science\", \"strength-conditioning\"]\ntags: [\"periodization\", \"block-periodization\", \"training-planning\", \"elite-athletes\"]\nfull-text-status: usable\nevidence-locator: page\ncross-linked: false\n---\n\n# Issurin 2016 — Benefits and limitations of block-periodized preparation\n\n> **Citation**\n> Issurin, V. B. (2016). Benefits and limitations of block periodized training approaches to athletes' preparation: A review. *Sports Medicine, 46*(3), 329–338. https://doi.org/10.1007/s40279-015-0425-5\n\n## Why it was promoted\n\nThis review distinguishes concentrated-unidirectional and multi-targeted block models and provides a useful conceptual vocabulary for discussing concentrated workloads and sequencing.\n\n## Research question\n\nWhat benefits, limitations, and sport-context boundaries are reported for two versions of block-periodized athlete preparation?\n\n## Population and context\n\n- The review emphasizes elite, subelite, and well-trained athletes across endurance, team, combat, dual, jumping, and strength/power settings. [p. 11]\n- It also discusses a smaller number of recreational-athlete studies.\n- It is a training-theory review, not evidence specific to collegiate American football.\n\n## Study design and methods\n\n- Narrative review contrasting concentrated-unidirectional and multi-targeted block-periodization models.\n- The article summarizes prior studies in tables but does not report a systematic search, eligibility process, formal risk-of-bias appraisal, or meta-analysis.\n- Consequently, the stated number and direction of supportive studies should be interpreted as an author-curated synthesis rather than a pooled comparative estimate.\n\n## Key findings\n\n- Concentrated-unidirectional programs in combat and team sports improved some fitness measures, but the reviewed studies did not report effects on team practice or competitive performance. [p. 6]\n- In an elite-swimmer example, specific strength and anaerobic capacity improved but swimming performance improved in only two of three athletes. [p. 6]\n- The author judged the concentrated-unidirectional evidence insufficient for broad implementation in high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation. [p. 6]\n- The review reports support from 28 studies for multi-targeted block programs, but the designs, populations, outcomes, and comparator quality varied. [p. 11]\n- The concentrated-unidirectional model was presented as better suited to sports dominated by a narrow target quality and as restricted when peak performance requires many interacting abilities. [p. 11]\n- Whose model is whose: the concentrated-unidirectional (CU) model is Verkhoshansky's; the multi-targeted model with 28-study support is Issurin's own A-T-R block model. [pp. 6-7, 11] So this review does not dispute Issurin's 2008 book; it repeats the book's critique of traditional planning. The real 2008-2016 difference is on lower-level athletes: the book (p. 33) says they do fine with medium concentration, while this review says \"no evidence that amateur athletes have any serious restrictions\" with highly concentrated block workloads. [p. 11] (Clarified 2026-09-27 during the Flint review of Issurin's book, checked against the paper pages.)\n\n## Limitations and applicability\n\n- Non-systematic study selection and absent risk-of-bias assessment create a substantial risk of selective emphasis.\n- Fitness improvements are not interchangeable with sport-specific performance, availability, or competitive outcomes. [p. 6]\n- Training models were not consistently isolated from total volume, intensity, exercise choice, coaching, or athlete history.\n- Evidence spans dissimilar sports and training levels; it does not supply validated block durations or effect estimates for collegiate American football.\n- The strong conclusion favoring multi-targeted block periodization is the author's synthesis and should not be treated as settled comparative evidence. [p. 11]\n\n## Practitioner interpretation\n\nUse block periodization as a planning hypothesis: concentrate compatible emphases when competition structure and athlete needs justify it, while maintaining the qualities and technical exposure that cannot safely decay. Judge the plan by sport-specific performance, availability, and individual response—not by the periodization label. Compare it prospectively with the program's own prior practice where feasible.\n\n## Evidence map\n\n| Claim | Locator | Evidence type |\n|---|---|---|\n| Review scope and distinction between two models | p. 4 | narrative framing |\n| Concentrated-unidirectional evidence and performance gaps | p. 6 | narrative synthesis |\n| Multi-targeted evidence claim and applicability boundaries | p. 11 | narrative synthesis/limitation |\n\n## Related knowledge\n\n- [[periodization]] — annual and phase planning\n- [[training-load-concentration]] — sequencing compatible emphases\n- [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]]: Supertraining 5.3.3's system of successive concentrated blocks; it cites this review's limits for team, combat and swimming preparation as the case against using that system there","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"2b840c5e-126e-4edc-b4e7-1fc7d9143e74","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:30:39.347Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"### ISSURIN brief\n---\ntype: paper-brief\nstatus: ingested\ncreated: 2026-07-31\nupdated: 2026-07-31\nsource: zotero\nzotero-item-key: PT9QTQ2I\nzotero-attachment-key: 46JPR6ZS\nzotero-version: 5193\ncitekey: \"issurinBenefitsLimitationsBlock2016\"\ndoi: \"10.1007/s40279-015-0425-5\"\ndomain: [\"sport-science\", \"strength-conditioning\"]\ntags: [\"periodization\", \"block-periodization\", \"training-planning\", \"elite-athletes\"]\nfull-text-status: usable\nevidence-locator: page\ncross-linked: false\n---\n\n# Issurin 2016 — Benefits and limitations of block-periodized preparation\n\n> **Citation**\n> Issurin, V. B. (2016). Benefits and limitations of block periodized training approaches to athletes' preparation: A review. *Sports Medicine, 46*(3), 329–338. https://doi.org/10.1007/s40279-015-0425-5\n\n## Why it was promoted\n\nThis review distinguishes concentrated-unidirectional and multi-targeted block models and provides a useful conceptual vocabulary for discussing concentrated workloads and sequencing.\n\n## Research question\n\nWhat benefits, limitations, and sport-context boundaries are reported for two versions of block-periodized athlete preparation?\n\n## Population and context\n\n- The review emphasizes elite, subelite, and well-trained athletes across endurance, team, combat, dual, jumping, and strength/power settings. [p. 11]\n- It also discusses a smaller number of recreational-athlete studies.\n- It is a training-theory review, not evidence specific to collegiate American football.\n\n## Study design and methods\n\n- Narrative review contrasting concentrated-unidirectional and multi-targeted block-periodization models.\n- The article summarizes prior studies in tables but does not report a systematic search, eligibility process, formal risk-of-bias appraisal, or meta-analysis.\n- Consequently, the stated number and direction of supportive studies should be interpreted as an author-curated synthesis rather than a pooled comparative estimate.\n\n## Key findings\n\n- Concentrated-unidirectional programs in combat and team sports improved some fitness measures, but the reviewed studies did not report effects on team practice or competitive performance. [p. 6]\n- In an elite-swimmer example, specific strength and anaerobic capacity improved but swimming performance improved in only two of three athletes. [p. 6]\n- The author judged the concentrated-unidirectional evidence insufficient for broad implementation in high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation. [p. 6]\n- The review reports support from 28 studies for multi-targeted block programs, but the designs, populations, outcomes, and comparator quality varied. [p. 11]\n- The concentrated-unidirectional model was presented as better suited to sports dominated by a narrow target quality and as restricted when peak performance requires many interacting abilities. [p. 11]\n- Whose model is whose: the concentrated-unidirectional (CU) model is Verkhoshansky's; the multi-targeted model with 28-study support is Issurin's own A-T-R block model. [pp. 6-7, 11] So this review does not dispute Issurin's 2008 book; it repeats the book's critique of traditional planning. The real 2008-2016 difference is on lower-level athletes: the book (p. 33) says they do fine with medium concentration, while this review says \"no evidence that amateur athletes have any serious restrictions\" with highly concentrated block workloads. [p. 11] (Clarified 2026-09-27 during the Flint review of Issurin's book, checked against the paper pages.)\n\n## Limitations and applicability\n\n- Non-systematic study selection and absent risk-of-bias assessment create a substantial risk of selective emphasis.\n- Fitness improvements are not interchangeable with sport-specific performance, availability, or competitive outcomes. [p. 6]\n- Training models were not consistently isolated from total volume, intensity, exercise choice, coaching, or athlete history.\n- Evidence spans dissimilar sports and training levels; it does not supply validated block durations or effect estimates for collegiate American football.\n- The strong conclusion favoring multi-targeted block periodization is the author's synthesis and should not be treated as settled comparative evidence. [p. 11]\n\n## Practitioner interpretation\n\nUse block periodization as a planning hypothesis: concentrate compatible emphases when competition structure and athlete needs justify it, while maintaining the qualities and technical exposure that cannot safely decay. Judge the plan by sport-specific performance, availability, and individual response—not by the periodization label. Compare it prospectively with the program's own prior practice where feasible.\n\n## Evidence map\n\n| Claim | Locator | Evidence type |\n|---|---|---|\n| Review scope and distinction between two models | p. 4 | narrative framing |\n| Concentrated-unidirectional evidence and performance gaps | p. 6 | narrative synthesis |\n| Multi-targeted evidence claim and applicability boundaries | p. 11 | narrative synthesis/limitation |\n\n## Related knowledge\n\n- [[periodization]] — annual and phase planning\n- [[training-load-concentration]] — sequencing compatible emphases\n- [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]]: Supertraining 5.3.3's system of successive concentrated blocks; it cites this review's limits for team, combat and swimming preparation as the case against using that system there","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9cc2b0a1-caff-40b8-bb94-c1fe8079d862","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
