x":1,"requestId":"req_011CffnCFgMgH22nwf51heix","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"39c23cf0-5cf4-40b5-bda8-7831f308609d","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:30:50.280Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"39c23cf0-5cf4-40b5-bda8-7831f308609d","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"9f0b5c05-18c3-490f-86c4-7f58fb0f08fd","agentId":"aeede644703c0347a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019QKUryPV9oJ3FdAWBRLE9k","type":"tool_result","content":"## accum\n14:# Accumulation Mesocycle: Up to Six Weeks for Deep Change, Three Weeks or Less to Refresh, 10-14 Days Late-Season\n26:sharply over time: the first 3 weeks produced a 5.9% average gain (the book prints this as a 1.93%/week\n27:improvement rate; arithmetically 5.9% over 3 weeks is 1.97%/week, recorded here as a source\n28:quirk, not corrected); the next 3 weeks added only 1.6% more (0.53%/week); the remaining 14\n29:weeks added only 0.25% and then 0.13% per week. Issurin's stated conclusion: the fitness\n31:weeks, and had only a minor effect for the next 14 weeks,\" directly supporting BPC's case for\n35:Issurin sets the following duration bands (PDF p. 115, printed p. 104): **up to six weeks**\n37:season, when athletes are less calendar-constrained); **three weeks or less** when training is\n## bondarchuk case\n14:# Case Study: Bondarchuk's Developmental/Competitive/Restorative Blocks and the 1988 Hammer Throw Podium\n22:**restorative** (active recovery, preparing for the next developmental block). Stated\n23:durations: developmental and competitive blocks are \"usually four weeks,\" restorative \"can\n50:(developmental/competitive/restorative) rather than ability type\n61:evidence that a 4/4/2-week block cadence \"works\" for hammer throw or any other event; it\n## allow-restoration\n14:# After a Concentrated Block, Unload and Wait: Gains Commonly Appear After a 1-3 Week Latency\n16:**What it is** — The authors warn: \"The system is not without its dangers, since concentrated loading is very stressful and, besides increasing the potential for overtraining, it can decrease motor qualities such as speed-strength. After a phase of emphasis on heavy strength training, there may be an increase in muscle stiffness and a loss of explosive strength, sporting skill or speed.\" Their rule, italicised in the original: \"a diminished loading and restorative period has to be observed after a given concentrated loading phase to allow the body to supercompensate to the desired level of preparedness.\" The timing: \"The gains elicited, for instance, by 4-8 week concentrated blocks of strength, speed-strength or speed training, may occur most commonly after a latency period of 1-3 weeks, so it is essential for the athlete not to lose patience and hasten into the next concentrated phase without adequate restoration. Not only does premature imposition of concentrated loading suppress the delayed training effect of the previous stage, but it also lowers the effectiveness of future training\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294, checked against the page image).\n20:**When to apply** — After any 4-8 week block that concentrates on one quality, plan a lower-load period before the next concentrated block. Inference: judge the block's result after the 1-3 week latency, not on the last day of loading. Eric's baseline: \"An annual plan is treated as a flexible roadmap, not a fixed contract\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 183, Inferred). Inference: the 1-3 week latency is a reason to leave slack between blocks in that roadmap, so a delayed gain is not cut short by the next block.\n22:**When NOT to apply** — The 1-3 week figure is \"most commonly,\" with no study named. It is a latency to the onset of gains. Chapter 6 gives a different number for a different quantity: the long-term delayed training effect \"was observed to last from 4-12 weeks during the long-term stages of strength preparation,\" its duration \"approximately equal to the duration of the strength training stage,\" and its appearance \"individualised\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364, checked against the page image). **Contested across the library:** Bompa & Haff (5th ed.), not Supertraining, write that \"Siff and Verkhoshansky (83) suggested that performance gains may occur 4 to 12 weeks after the cessation of the concentrated loading phase\" (Bompa & Haff 5e, PDF idx 60 / printed p. 49), and the Bompa entry [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]] repeats this as a 4-12 week delay. Bompa & Haff also say that \"the greater the magnitude and duration of the concentrated loading phase, the more time needed for fatigue to dissipate and performance to improve\" (same page, refs 83, 91, 103, 104). Inference: read as a 4-12 week wait before gains, the Bompa & Haff sentence matches neither Supertraining statement: 5.3.3 gives a 1-3 week latency to gains (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294), and Chapter 6 gives 4-12 weeks as how long the delayed effect lasts (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364). Read as the window in which gains show, it is close to the Chapter 6 figure. Fig 6.31 on that page (checked against the page image) shows the timing: the trained qualities (F1-F3) are depressed during the concentrated loading stage T1 and climb during T2, which begins where T1 ends (dashed line). The other 4-12 week figures in the book are also durations: Chapter 6 calls unidirectional training \"for prolonged stages (4-12 weeks)\" very effective (PDF p. 381 / printed p. 370, text layer), and the Addendum says \"the tendency has been for the stages to last anywhere from 4-12 weeks,\" with the delayed effect's duration \"equal to the duration of the stage\" (PDF p. 536 / printed p. 525, text layer). Use the onset figure for when to expect a result and the duration figure for how long it may hold. Chapter 6 also qualifies the warning: during the emergence of the delayed effect \"it is relatively easy and painless for the body to change to intensive loading, but it will react negatively to extensive work\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364), so the restorative period is about volume, not a full stop.\n24:**Example** — The authors' own: after a 4-8 week concentrated strength block, the gains \"may occur most commonly after a latency period of 1-3 weeks\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294). Inference: an off-season strength block ending in late March would be judged in April, and the speed-emphasis block that follows would open at lower volume rather than at full load.\n26:**Related** — [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[framework--acute-delayed-particular-cumulative-training-effect-taxonomy]] (4.1.2, the delayed training effect), [[case-study--deniskin-depth-jumps-after-resistance-and-rest-as-a-training-means]] (rest weeks between stages), [[framework--fitness-fatigue-modeling-and-taper-timing]], [[claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation]], [[claim--delayed-strength-training-effect-lag-before-gains-appear]] (4.1.1, speed-strength up 18-25% after a 10-day rest, PDF p. 213; an in-library case for a short latency), [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]]\n## adaptive-reconstruction CAR\n28:The book gives two different definitions of \"Current Adaptation Reserves\" (CAR) for\n31:Adaptation Reserves (CAR),\" while the Fig 1.39 caption on printed 84 states \"The Deep\n32:Adaptation Energy is often referred to as the Current Adaptation Reserves (CAR) by Russian\n47:**When to apply** — This library already carries the supercompensation model in\n53:glycogen-only overshoot finding) and the CAR terminology, which the Bompa entry does not\n73:CAR/adaptive-reconstruction terminology).\n## vo2max\n14:# VO2 Max on Its Own Does Not Decide Endurance Results: Equal VO2 Max, Different Results; Stable VO2 Max, Rising Results; Falling VO2 Max in the Competition Period\n19:3. VO2 max falls significantly in the competition period in cyclists, swimmers, skiers, skaters, middle-distance runners and hockey players (citations for each, PDF p. 259 / printed p. 248), and its correlation with results falls from \"R = 0.7 during the preparation period\" to \"0.4 during the competitive period\" (Vasiliev & Trunin 1974; Melenberg 1981; PDF p. 260 / printed p. 249, verified against the page image).\n## conditions CU 305\n16:**What it is** — The authors set out the working conditions for the concentrated phases of the conjugate sequence system (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294, checked against the page image):\n17:1. **Scale and duration.** \"Unidirectional emphasis on qualities such as strength or endurance may be used not only in individual workouts but also during each microcycle (Kudelin, 1980; Budokho et al, 1978; Mironenko, 1980). Research has shown that unidirectional training over periods as long as 4-12 weeks can be very effective, especially if the intensity is gradually increased for each form of loading in the given cycle.\"\n25:**When to apply** — Planning a concentrated block for an advanced athlete: fill it with several different exercises that share one emphasis, keep each one moderate in intensity, and expect the month's volume to be about a quarter of the year's. Eric's baseline: \"Training residual effects set the retraining cadence: aerobic capacity and max strength both need re-exposure roughly every 30 days; speed needs re-exposure roughly every 5 days\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 184, Inferred). Inference: a 4-12 week unidirectional block runs past those windows for the qualities it does not train, so the baseline's retention dose would still be needed alongside it; the authors do not address retention of other qualities here.\n## bompa threshold\n14:# Progressive Overload and the Threshold of Adaptation\n16:**What it is** — Adaptation follows a load-dependent sequence with three outcomes: increasing, varied stimulus ⇒ adaptation ⇒ performance improvement; a *standard* (unchanging) load ⇒ plateau ⇒ stagnation; an *excessive* load ⇒ maladaptation ⇒ decline (overreaching/overtraining). Improvement requires systematically crossing the current \"threshold of adaptation\" by raising intensity, volume, or frequency — then doing so again once the body has adapted.\n18:**Why it works** — Training is beneficial only insofar as it overloads the system enough to demand adaptation. A stimulus below threshold provokes no change; a sustained intolerable stimulus injures or overtrains. Bompa stresses that overload must be paired with **variation** to keep maximizing adaptation rather than habituating.\n20:**When to apply** — The default logic for advancing any program; diagnosing a plateau (\"is the load still progressing, or has it gone standard?\"); justifying planned increases over a block.\n22:**When NOT to apply** — Not a license for linear, monotonic load increase indefinitely — that path leads to the maladaptation branch. The *rate* and *form* of progression must respect recovery and training age. Overload without adequate recovery is how you cross into overreaching. Pairs with [[principle--supercompensation-cycle]] (recovery side) and is bounded by [[heuristic--fatigue-fitness-3to1-ratio]].","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"fd9b3f75-741f-4e4a-9dfe-06af453823ab","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:30:50.794Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"## accum\n14:# Accumulation Mesocycle: Up to Six Weeks for Deep Change, Three Weeks or Less to Refresh, 10-14 Days Late-Season\n26:sharply over time: the first 3 weeks produced a 5.9% average gain (the book prints this as a 1.93%/week\n27:improvement rate; arithmetically 5.9% over 3 weeks is 1.97%/week, recorded here as a source\n28:quirk, not corrected); the next 3 weeks added only 1.6% more (0.53%/week); the remaining 14\n29:weeks added only 0.25% and then 0.13% per week. Issurin's stated conclusion: the fitness\n31:weeks, and had only a minor effect for the next 14 weeks,\" directly supporting BPC's case for\n35:Issurin sets the following duration bands (PDF p. 115, printed p. 104): **up to six weeks**\n37:season, when athletes are less calendar-constrained); **three weeks or less** when training is\n## bondarchuk case\n14:# Case Study: Bondarchuk's Developmental/Competitive/Restorative Blocks and the 1988 Hammer Throw Podium\n22:**restorative** (active recovery, preparing for the next developmental block). Stated\n23:durations: developmental and competitive blocks are \"usually four weeks,\" restorative \"can\n50:(developmental/competitive/restorative) rather than ability type\n61:evidence that a 4/4/2-week block cadence \"works\" for hammer throw or any other event; it\n## allow-restoration\n14:# After a Concentrated Block, Unload and Wait: Gains Commonly Appear After a 1-3 Week Latency\n16:**What it is** — The authors warn: \"The system is not without its dangers, since concentrated loading is very stressful and, besides increasing the potential for overtraining, it can decrease motor qualities such as speed-strength. After a phase of emphasis on heavy strength training, there may be an increase in muscle stiffness and a loss of explosive strength, sporting skill or speed.\" Their rule, italicised in the original: \"a diminished loading and restorative period has to be observed after a given concentrated loading phase to allow the body to supercompensate to the desired level of preparedness.\" The timing: \"The gains elicited, for instance, by 4-8 week concentrated blocks of strength, speed-strength or speed training, may occur most commonly after a latency period of 1-3 weeks, so it is essential for the athlete not to lose patience and hasten into the next concentrated phase without adequate restoration. Not only does premature imposition of concentrated loading suppress the delayed training effect of the previous stage, but it also lowers the effectiveness of future training\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294, checked against the page image).\n20:**When to apply** — After any 4-8 week block that concentrates on one quality, plan a lower-load period before the next concentrated block. Inference: judge the block's result after the 1-3 week latency, not on the last day of loading. Eric's baseline: \"An annual plan is treated as a flexible roadmap, not a fixed contract\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 183, Inferred). Inference: the 1-3 week latency is a reason to leave slack between blocks in that roadmap, so a delayed gain is not cut short by the next block.\n22:**When NOT to apply** — The 1-3 week figure is \"most commonly,\" with no study named. It is a latency to the onset of gains. Chapter 6 gives a different number for a different quantity: the long-term delayed training effect \"was observed to last from 4-12 weeks during the long-term stages of strength preparation,\" its duration \"approximately equal to the duration of the strength training stage,\" and its appearance \"individualised\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364, checked against the page image). **Contested across the library:** Bompa & Haff (5th ed.), not Supertraining, write that \"Siff and Verkhoshansky (83) suggested that performance gains may occur 4 to 12 weeks after the cessation of the concentrated loading phase\" (Bompa & Haff 5e, PDF idx 60 / printed p. 49), and the Bompa entry [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]] repeats this as a 4-12 week delay. Bompa & Haff also say that \"the greater the magnitude and duration of the concentrated loading phase, the more time needed for fatigue to dissipate and performance to improve\" (same page, refs 83, 91, 103, 104). Inference: read as a 4-12 week wait before gains, the Bompa & Haff sentence matches neither Supertraining statement: 5.3.3 gives a 1-3 week latency to gains (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294), and Chapter 6 gives 4-12 weeks as how long the delayed effect lasts (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364). Read as the window in which gains show, it is close to the Chapter 6 figure. Fig 6.31 on that page (checked against the page image) shows the timing: the trained qualities (F1-F3) are depressed during the concentrated loading stage T1 and climb during T2, which begins where T1 ends (dashed line). The other 4-12 week figures in the book are also durations: Chapter 6 calls unidirectional training \"for prolonged stages (4-12 weeks)\" very effective (PDF p. 381 / printed p. 370, text layer), and the Addendum says \"the tendency has been for the stages to last anywhere from 4-12 weeks,\" with the delayed effect's duration \"equal to the duration of the stage\" (PDF p. 536 / printed p. 525, text layer). Use the onset figure for when to expect a result and the duration figure for how long it may hold. Chapter 6 also qualifies the warning: during the emergence of the delayed effect \"it is relatively easy and painless for the body to change to intensive loading, but it will react negatively to extensive work\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364), so the restorative period is about volume, not a full stop.\n24:**Example** — The authors' own: after a 4-8 week concentrated strength block, the gains \"may occur most commonly after a latency period of 1-3 weeks\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294). Inference: an off-season strength block ending in late March would be judged in April, and the speed-emphasis block that follows would open at lower volume rather than at full load.\n26:**Related** — [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[framework--acute-delayed-particular-cumulative-training-effect-taxonomy]] (4.1.2, the delayed training effect), [[case-study--deniskin-depth-jumps-after-resistance-and-rest-as-a-training-means]] (rest weeks between stages), [[framework--fitness-fatigue-modeling-and-taper-timing]], [[claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation]], [[claim--delayed-strength-training-effect-lag-before-gains-appear]] (4.1.1, speed-strength up 18-25% after a 10-day rest, PDF p. 213; an in-library case for a short latency), [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]]\n## adaptive-reconstruction CAR\n28:The book gives two different definitions of \"Current Adaptation Reserves\" (CAR) for\n31:Adaptation Reserves (CAR),\" while the Fig 1.39 caption on printed 84 states \"The Deep\n32:Adaptation Energy is often referred to as the Current Adaptation Reserves (CAR) by Russian\n47:**When to apply** — This library already carries the supercompensation model in\n53:glycogen-only overshoot finding) and the CAR terminology, which the Bompa entry does not\n73:CAR/adaptive-reconstruction terminology).\n## vo2max\n14:# VO2 Max on Its Own Does Not Decide Endurance Results: Equal VO2 Max, Different Results; Stable VO2 Max, Rising Results; Falling VO2 Max in the Competition Period\n19:3. VO2 max falls significantly in the competition period in cyclists, swimmers, skiers, skaters, middle-distance runners and hockey players (citations for each, PDF p. 259 / printed p. 248), and its correlation with results falls from \"R = 0.7 during the preparation period\" to \"0.4 during the competitive period\" (Vasiliev & Trunin 1974; Melenberg 1981; PDF p. 260 / printed p. 249, verified against the page image).\n## conditions CU 305\n16:**What it is** — The authors set out the working conditions for the concentrated phases of the conjugate sequence system (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294, checked against the page image):\n17:1. **Scale and duration.** \"Unidirectional emphasis on qualities such as strength or endurance may be used not only in individual workouts but also during each microcycle (Kudelin, 1980; Budokho et al, 1978; Mironenko, 1980). Research has shown that unidirectional training over periods as long as 4-12 weeks can be very effective, especially if the intensity is gradually increased for each form of loading in the given cycle.\"\n25:**When to apply** — Planning a concentrated block for an advanced athlete: fill it with several different exercises that share one emphasis, keep each one moderate in intensity, and expect the month's volume to be about a quarter of the year's. Eric's baseline: \"Training residual effects set the retraining cadence: aerobic capacity and max strength both need re-exposure roughly every 30 days; speed needs re-exposure roughly every 5 days\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 184, Inferred). Inference: a 4-12 week unidirectional block runs past those windows for the qualities it does not train, so the baseline's retention dose would still be needed alongside it; the authors do not address retention of other qualities here.\n## bompa threshold\n14:# Progressive Overload and the Threshold of Adaptation\n16:**What it is** — Adaptation follows a load-dependent sequence with three outcomes: increasing, varied stimulus ⇒ adaptation ⇒ performance improvement; a *standard* (unchanging) load ⇒ plateau ⇒ stagnation; an *excessive* load ⇒ maladaptation ⇒ decline (overreaching/overtraining). Improvement requires systematically crossing the current \"threshold of adaptation\" by raising intensity, volume, or frequency — then doing so again once the body has adapted.\n18:**Why it works** — Training is beneficial only insofar as it overloads the system enough to demand adaptation. A stimulus below threshold provokes no change; a sustained intolerable stimulus injures or overtrains. Bompa stresses that overload must be paired with **variation** to keep maximizing adaptation rather than habituating.\n20:**When to apply** — The default logic for advancing any program; diagnosing a plateau (\"is the load still progressing, or has it gone standard?\"); justifying planned increases over a block.\n22:**When NOT to apply** — Not a license for linear, monotonic load increase indefinitely — that path leads to the maladaptation branch. The *rate* and *form* of progression must respect recovery and training age. Overload without adequate recovery is how you cross into overreaching. Pairs with [[principle--supercompensation-cycle]] (recovery side) and is bounded by [[heuristic--fatigue-fitness-3to1-ratio]].","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"39c23cf0-5cf4-40b5-bda8-7831f308609d","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
