{"parentUuid":"9367c784-4fb9-40ef-93e9-29e257047350","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"aeede644703c0347a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffnZFjRnWMfR9hURuFSu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11801,"cache_read_input_tokens":203971,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":11801,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":1935,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffnZ5mPVB2QnXeoD9ofo","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"2f1affee-48e4-4c62-9e71-bddc2a3c7406","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:35:27.289Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"2f1affee-48e4-4c62-9e71-bddc2a3c7406","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"aeede644703c0347a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffnZFjRnWMfR9hURuFSu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012FsJxgtq2HPNtG5baZChDK","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch6b_opus.md","content":"# Review: Supertraining Chapter 6, batch 6b (sections 6.4 to 6.8.2)\n\nReviewer: Claude Opus, independent falsification pass, 2026-10-03.\nScope: the 16 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch6b.txt`.\nSource: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, PDF idx 347-362 (printed 336-351), plus PDF 305 and 381.\n\nMethod: I extracted the text layer for PDF 305, 347-362 and 381. I rendered and opened at 200 dpi PDF 348, 349, 351, 353, 354, 357, 358, 359, 360, 361 and 362, and the middle of 381. I also cropped Fig 6.22 and both panels of Fig 6.23 at 400 dpi and read values with pixel measurements (Fig 6.23 A: 11.12 px per 1%; B: 11.13 px per 1%; 64.3 to 64.6 px per week). I checked all 19 cited `method-baseline.md` lines (27, 30, 32, 59, 64, 75, 101, 105, 106, 115, 116, 117, 123, 151, 153, 171, 183, 185, 204). Every quote and tag matches. All 62 wikilink targets resolve. Each file has exactly six em dashes, all in the section headers, and no wikilink is wrapped across lines. Renders are in `staging/_renders/r6b_*.png`.\n\n**PDF 359:** The writer read this page from the text layer only. I opened the page image, and the text layer matches it word for word for every quote used. (The adaptation definition, \"adaptation cannot be considered an equilibrium process ...\", is italic in the original.) The fixer can change \"text layer only\" to \"checked against the page image\" in the four entries that cite PDF 359.\n\n**PDF 381:** I checked the image. The text says \"prolonged stages (4-12 weeks)\" for unidirectional training and \"up to 2 months\" for concentrated loading.\n\n## Entries\n\n| File | Verdict | Problem (entry vs source, with page) | Exact correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| framework--logical-scheme-of-training-management-load-fitness-state-external-interactions | FIX | (1) The entry says \"Athlete's fitness state (shaded box A) ... Athlete's external interactions (shaded box B)\". The Fig 6.12 image (PDF 348 / p.337) shows box A as the whole shaded band {Training Load, Training Potential of Loading, Athlete's Fitness State}. Box B is the larger shaded region {Athlete's Fitness State, Training Effect of Loading, Athlete's External Interactions}, with fitness state in both. The text's subsystem list is right; only the figure walk-through mislabels the boxes. (2) Why it works says \"fitness state is the only route from load to result, so both ... links must be measured\" and presents this as the authors' reason. PDF 347-348 gives the one-way chain and the two control levels, but neither \"only route\" nor \"must be measured\" is stated. Checked and correct: all the 6.4 quotes (PDF 347-348), the Fig 6.12 box names and the arrow from Sports Competition and Laboratory Tests into Assessment of Effect, the \"lower the measures for the first one (A)\" quote, and baseline lines 64 and 32. | (1) Replace the parentheticals with: \"the shaded band A (Training Load, Training Potential of Loading, Athlete's Fitness State) and the shaded region B (Athlete's Fitness State, Training Effect of Loading, Athlete's External Interactions), which overlap at fitness state\". (2) Change the Why-it-works lead to: \"The authors set out a one-way chain (load, fitness state, external interactions) and control at two levels (PDF 348). Inference: fitness state is therefore the only route from load to result, so both links need measuring.\" |\n| heuristic--build-training-theory-from-interacting-complexes-and-coach-scientist-cooperation | GO | Checked: the \"individual fragments of research\" opening, the \"All too often ... single factor\", \"very rarely\" and specialist quotes (PDF 349 / p.338), \"cannot be condemned ... 'their' types of sport\", the coach-scientist passage and the VO2max fall in the competition period (PDF 350 / p.339), and every Fig 6.13 box against the image (PDF 349). The linked VO2max claim does carry the competition-period fall (PDF 259-260). Baseline lines 27 and 30 (Explicit) match. The When NOT section is substantive. `untested` is right. | None. |\n| heuristic--arousal-optimum-is-individual-so-fit-restoration-to-personality-and-task-complexity | FIX | (1) Fig 6.15 description: the entry says \"a narrow spike A at low arousal, and spikes B and C near high arousal.\" In the image (PDF 351 / p.340), B is not a separate spike. It is a second hump on the right-hand descending limb of the broad inverted-U, which matches the text's \"'second wind' phenomenon (curve B)\". Only A and C are separate narrow spikes. (2) Borrowed models: the entry correctly names Yerkes and Dodson as the source of the curve. The introvert/extrovert arousal claim in point 2 is also a borrowed idea, but the book names no source for it, and the entry lists it under \"The authors' points\" with nothing to say it is not their own finding. Checked and correct: every other quote on PDF 351 and the PDF 352 carry-over (\"overstress\", \"supercompensation\"), the \"(Siff, 1990)\" note on PDF 350, baseline line 153, the Example's golf, gymnastics and pole vaulting, and `untested`. | (1) Replace the Fig 6.15 sentence with: \"one broad inverted-U over 'low / optimal / high' arousal; a narrow spike A at low arousal; a second hump B on the right-hand slope of the broad curve ('second wind'); and a narrow spike C at high arousal.\" (2) In point 2, add: \"The book gives no source for the personality difference; it is presented as a general claim ('appears'), not as the authors' finding.\" Optional provenance notes (Eysenck for the arousal and personality idea, the 1908 Yerkes-Dodson task) need a checked citation before they are added. |\n| heuristic--expectancy-motivation-break-distant-goals-into-attainable-steps | GO | Checked against the PDF 353 / p.342 image: the \"Vroom's Expectancy Theory of Motivation\" naming, \"(Tellier, 1978)\", the V and B definitions, F = V x B, the -5 to +5 and 0 to 1 scales, the sum over outcomes, \"Detailed examples ... outside the scope of this book\", and the leader quote. The model is named as Vroom's, and the scales are reported as given, not as the authors' finding. The entry flags the single-product versus sum wording correctly. Baseline line 171 (Inferred) matches. Example is labelled Inference. `untested` is right. | None. Optional: say that the two-factor form and the scales are as reported through Tellier, so later readers do not cite them as Vroom's original formulation. |\n| framework--situational-leadership-coach-matches-style-to-athlete-maturity | GO | Checked against the Fig 6.19 image (PDF 354 / p.343). Relationship behaviour is on the vertical axis and task behaviour on the horizontal. The quadrants are S3 Participating (top left), S2 Selling (top right), S4 Delegating (bottom left) and S1 Telling (bottom right). There is a bell curve, and the maturity strip runs M4 to M1 from left to right, so M1 pairs with S1. The quotes (\"ought to be implemented according to each situation\", the maturity list, \"low, moderate or high (M1, M2, M3 or M4)\", \"hence the shape of the curve\") all match. The model is credited to Hersey and Blanchard, and the three-words-for-four-codes gap is flagged. Baseline line 204 matches. `untested` is right. | None. Minor: the Why-it-works clause \"a follower low in experience ... needs direction\" is the model's logic read off the figure; prefixing \"Inference:\" would be cleaner. |\n| framework--three-sport-groups-acyclic-cyclic-complex-for-programming | FIX | When NOT says \"the authors' own call to 'further subdivide' applies\" to positions within the complex group. On PDF 357 / p.346 (image) the call (\"One should further subdivide the sports in each of these broad groups\") comes after the acyclic and cyclic bullets and before \"Finally, one should identify a third group\". It is made for the two broad groups only, and the complex group is never subdivided. Checked and correct: all the 6.7 quotes (PDF 356-357), including \"the greater part being derived from glycolytic reactions\" (in the third-group paragraph); Farfel (1969); the 100 m example; baseline lines 115, 116 and 117, with quotes and tags matching. The source-against-baseline contrast on football is fairly stated. | Change the sentence to: \"Inference: within the complex group, positions differ enough ... that the group label decides little on its own. The authors call for subdivision only within the acyclic and cyclic groups (PDF 357); extending it to the complex group is this entry's inference.\" |\n| principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-the-unit-and-planned-dips-are-not-errors | FIX | (1) Why it works: \"The authors' reason is that the effects they care about ... unfold over weeks, so judging each microcycle against the previous one misreads them.\" PDF 358 / p.347 gives \"modern sporting requirements\" and \"common sense\" as the basis and calls for \"An extensive study of ... long-term adaptation\". The weeks-long-effects mechanism is the writer's. (2) When NOT contains process notes: \"Batch 6a (sections 6.1 to 6.3 ...) may carry an overlapping entry ...\". The brief forbids review or process history in entries. (3) Missing tension: the 6.2 entry `framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap` quotes the book calling the training session \"the fundamental unit\" (PDF 327 / p.316), while 6.8.1 says the fundamental unit \"should be the mesocycle\". Checked and correct: the three bulleted recommendations, the Folbrot and Weigert citations and the verdict quote (PDF 358 image), and baseline lines 59 and 106. | (1) Rewrite as: \"The authors' stated basis is 'modern sporting requirements' and 'common sense' (PDF 358); they give no study. Inference: the effects they go on to describe (long-term adaptation, the delayed rise after concentrated loading) unfold over weeks, so microcycle-to-microcycle comparison would misread them.\" (2) Delete the batch 6a sentence. Replace it with: \"The book calls the training session 'the fundamental unit' of periodisation in 6.2 (PDF 327 / p.316) and the mesocycle the fundamental unit of training in 6.8.1; the first is a structural unit, the second a planning unit. Inference: the book does not reconcile the wording.\" Link [[framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap]]. |\n| claim--four-observations-on-long-term-speed-strength-adaptation | FIX | **Figure misread under a false verification claim.** The entry says it checked Fig 6.22 \"against the page image and a high-resolution crop\" and that \"in the image A is close to a straight line over the three years drawn, with no visible peak\". It treats this as figure-against-text. My 400 dpi crop of PDF 358 / p.347 shows graph A's rise shrinking each year: about 220 px in Year 1, 145 px in Year 2 and 65 px in Year 3 (slopes of roughly 0.39, 0.25 and 0.11). That is a decelerating curve, consistent with the text's \"parabola whose peak is eventually reached with time\" (PDF 359). No peak is reached within the three years drawn, which the text does not claim either. The contradiction does not exist. Checked and correct: all four conclusions word for word (PDF 358 image), the B and C assignment to bi-cyclic and mono-cyclic, the A and D descriptions, the endurance caveat (PDF 359, now image-checked), and baseline lines 64 and 185. `supported` is right. | Replace \"a thick, nearly straight rising line A\" with \"a thick rising line A whose slope shrinks each year (steepest in Year 1, flattest in Year 3)\". Delete the \"Figure against text\" sentences in When NOT. Replace them with: \"Graph A flattens year by year, in line with the text's parabola (PDF 359); no peak is drawn within the three years.\" Change the PDF 359 locators from \"text layer only\" to \"checked against the page image\". |\n| claim--compensatory-adaptation-is-temporary-and-precedes-stable-adaptation | FIX | (1) The same Fig 6.22 misread: When NOT says \"Fig 6.22's graph A looks nearly straight over the three years drawn, not parabolic\". As shown above, A decelerates and matches the parabola. (2) Why it works says endurance adaptation \"has to be taken much further\". On PDF 359 that phrase is about research, not adaptation: \"The research done by Folbrot (1941), Selye ... and others has to be taken much further in its application to ... especially endurance sports.\" (3) \"Page 359 was read from the text layer only\": now checked against the image, and every quote matches. Checked and correct: the compensatory and pre-adaptation quotes, Kuznyetsov 1979, the Bauer and Bernstein definition, the heterochronicity bullets, \"parabola\", \"genetically predetermined\" and CAR (all PDF 359 image). The genetic-limit inference is labelled. | (1) Delete \"and Fig 6.22's graph A looks nearly straight over the three years drawn, not parabolic\". Optionally add: \"Graph A's slope shrinks each year, consistent with the parabola.\" (2) Change to: \"... and that research on adaptation (Folbrot, Selye) 'has to be taken much further' for endurance sports; until then they assume 'the same, above-mentioned chief mechanisms ...'\". (3) Change the locator to \"checked against the page image\" and delete the \"Page 359 was read from the text layer only\" sentence. |\n| principle--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window | GO | Checked: the CAR quotes on PDF 359 (image) and PDF 360 (image), Letunov 1952, Prokor 1959 (the book's spelling), the re-adaptation definition at the top of PDF 360, Matveyev 1964, and Beckman 1961 and Yakovlev 1977. The Chapter 1 two-definitions point is confirmed in the vault `claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation`. Baseline line 75 matches (Explicit Q2, Q2a). `supported` is right. When NOT is substantive. | None required. Minor: the PDF 359 locator can now read \"checked against the page image\". The Bompa entry describes a *standard* load leading to a plateau, not a below-threshold one, so \"(below threshold, plateau ...)\" should read \"(an unchanging load, plateau; an excessive one, maladaptation)\". |\n| framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation | FIX | **Fig 6.23 values: all verified.** Panel A ends at about 144, 128, 114, 108 and 105%. A hump peaks at about 113% near week 8.8, then falls to the lowest end value; by end position and label order (maximum strength, absolute strength, speed) that is speed, and the entry's \"by that reading\" caveat is fair. Panel B lows run from about 83% to 96% at weeks 4-5. Curves cross back above 100% at about weeks 7-9 and end at about 157, 142-143, 129 (peak of about 130-131 near week 16.5), 120 and 114%. Dashed lines sit at weeks 6 and 12. Which curve dips deepest cannot be settled because the curves cross at weeks 7-9, as the entry says. **Problem 1:** When NOT has \"Figure against text: the figure's depressed phase (below 100% until about weeks 7-9) runs longer than the 3-4 week concentrated block\". This compares dip duration with block length, which are different quantities. The text on the same page says the concentrated volume sits \"in the first half of the training phase\" (PDF 360 image), and a dip through weeks 7-9 of 18 is that first half. Indicators are expected to stay low until volume drops. **Problem 2:** The Issurin (2016) critique is stated accurately: insufficient evidence for broad use in combat, team-sport and swimming preparation, with fitness gains but no reported effect on team practice or competition (p.6), and better suited to narrow target-quality sports (p.11), all as in the vault brief. But the entry does not say whose model is being criticised; per the brief, it is Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model, not Issurin's A-T-R. **Problem 3:** \"Not for novices or medium-level athletes, by the authors' own scope\". PDF 360 says the method \"was devised for highly qualified athletes\"; it does not forbid it for medium-level athletes. And 6.9.5 (`claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes`, PDF 371) says \"both methods are successful in training athletes of medium qualification\". Checked and correct: all the PDF 360-361 quotes, Komarova 1984 (\"he errs\"), and baseline line 105 (Inferred, \"Not claimed in the interview\"). `contested` stands, for the Issurin reason. | (1) Replace the Fig 6.23 \"figure against text\" sentence with: \"Panel B's depression lasts until about weeks 7-9 of 18, which fits the text's 'concentrated in the first half of the training phase' (PDF 360). The figure marks 6-week intervals (dashed lines at weeks 6 and 12) and shows no 3-4 week blocks or 7-10 day pauses, so it does not illustrate the block durations given on the same page. Its x-axis ends at 18 weeks; the text's period is 18-22 weeks.\" (2) Change \"Issurin (2016) judged the concentrated-unidirectional evidence ...\" to \"Issurin (2016) judged the evidence for Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model ...\". (3) Replace the last sentence with: \"The authors devised it for highly qualified athletes (PDF 360); in 6.9.5 they say both forms work at medium qualification (PDF 371), so it is not required there.\" Add the reciprocal link [[claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes]]. |\n| heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery | FIX | **`contested` is justified, but the conflict mixes up different quantities.** Sorted by what each number measures: (a) **Concentrated block or period length:** 3-4 weeks (PDF 360 / p.349, image); \"4-8 week concentrated blocks\" (PDF 305 / p.294); \"a limited period (of up to 2 months)\" (PDF 381 / p.370, image). These are the same quantity, so the conflict is real. (b) **Unidirectional training stage length:** \"unidirectional training over periods as long as 4-12 weeks\" (PDF 305) and \"prolonged stages (4-12 weeks)\" (PDF 381). This is a stage of one emphasis, not a concentrated block. (c) **Delayed-effect duration:** 4-12 weeks (PDF 375, recorded in the vault `heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next`). (d) **Latency to gains:** 1-3 weeks (PDF 305). (e) **Stage to exhaust the CAR:** 18-22 weeks (PDF 360). (f) **Upper limit for one primary emphasis:** months, by quality (staging 6.9.4 `claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality`, PDF 370-373). The entry files the 4-12 week figure under \"Contested inside the book (concentrated block duration)\", next to the 4-8 weeks, as if both were block lengths. Checked and correct: every numbered quote (PDF 360 image), Zhikharevich 1976 and Sirenko 1980, the arithmetic (17-21 and 11-15 weeks), and baseline line 183. The Issurin (six weeks) and Bondarchuk (four-week blocks) Related notes match the vault files. | Rewrite the When NOT conflict paragraph as: \"**Contested inside the book (concentrated block length).** 3-4 weeks here (PDF 360 / p.349); '4-8 week concentrated blocks' in 5.3.3 (PDF 305 / p.294); 'a limited period (of up to 2 months)' in 6.12.2 (PDF 381 / p.370). **Different quantities, not part of the conflict:** 4-12 weeks is the length of a unidirectional training *stage* (PDF 305, 381); 4-12 weeks is also how long the delayed training effect lasts (PDF 375, see [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]]); 1-3 weeks is the latency to gains (PDF 305); 18-22 weeks is the stage needed to exhaust the CAR (PDF 360).\" Keep the \"three 3-4 week blocks give about 9-12 weeks\" reconciliation, labelled Inference, as a link between block length (a) and stage length (b). Add as Inference: \"5.3.3 and 6.12.2 both define loading as concentrated by one month's volume (23-25% of the annual volume, PDF 305, 381), which makes the month the working unit; 'up to 2 months' reads as a ceiling.\" Add the link [[claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality]]. |\n| heuristic--restorative-stage-length-scales-with-homeostatic-disturbance | GO | Checked: the full restorative-stage quote against the PDF 361 / p.350 image, the \"after the volume of loading is decreased\" quote (PDF 360), the 1-3 week latency after 4-8 week blocks (PDF 305), and the 7-10 day pause (PDF 360). The Bompa & Haff quote matches the vault `allow-restoration` entry word for word. Baseline line 151 (Inferred) matches. The GPP mechanism is correctly marked as not given. The Example is labelled Inference. `supported` is right. | None. Minor: \"as quoted in the 5.3.3 entry above\" should read \"as quoted in [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]]\", since \"above\" means nothing in a standalone note. |\n| claim--bondarchuk-50-percent-more-volume-did-not-prolong-training-effect | FIX | (1) Process note in the entry body: \"The Table 6.1 and 6.2.2 material it points to is in batch 6a's pages (PDF pp. 334-336), not checked here.\" The brief forbids review or process history. (I confirmed that 6.2.2 starts on PDF 334 and Table 6.1 is referenced on PDF 335.) (2) Why it works opens \"Bondarchuk's study as reported does not test intensity at all; the comparison with intensity is the authors' reading\". That is a correct observation, but it is the writer's, presented without a label. Checked and correct: the full quote, including the italic \"the importance of paying careful attention to zones of loading intensity\" (PDF 361 image), and baseline line 105 (Inferred, \"Not claimed in the interview\"). The tension with the PDF 360 \"more extensive ... the longer the new functional level is maintained\" claim is accurate and labelled. `untested` is acceptable: one group, reported second-hand, with Bondarchuk's own \"strictly\" caveat. | (1) Replace the batch sentence with: \"The 6.2.2 intensity-zone material and Table 6.1 it points to are at PDF pp. 334-336 / printed pp. 323-325.\" (2) Prefix \"Inference:\" to the first sentence of Why it works. |\n| principle--functional-specialisation-is-a-primary-emphasis-not-an-exclusive-energy-system | FIX | (1) Why it works: \"For the cyclic side they point to Chapter 4's argument that endurance rests on muscular as well as respiratory qualities.\" PDF 362 / p.351 (image) points only to 2.2.3 and 5.4.3, and supports the cyclic point with Zhurbinoi (1978). There is no Chapter 4 pointer on the page. (2) \"(Zhurbinoi, 1978)\" is attached to \"strength preparedness, mainly in the form of strength-endurance, plays an important role\". In the source, the citation closes the *next* sentence (\"Research shows that, with a relatively moderate increase in muscular strength ... (Zhurbinoi, 1978)\"). Checked and correct: the 6.8.2 quotes (PDF 361-362 images), the \"classification scheme of 6.5\" note (a correct catch: the scheme is in 6.7), Sargent 1926, Henry 1951, Margaria 1963, and the triple jump example. Baseline line 123 (Inferred) and line 116 (Goals 1 and 2 are alactic and aerobic) match. | (1) Rewrite as: \"For the cyclic side the authors cite Zhurbinoi (1978) on strength-endurance (PDF 362). Inference: Chapter 4's argument that endurance rests on muscular as well as respiratory qualities (see Related) supports the same point.\" (2) Quote both sentences with the citation where the source puts it, or write: \"strength preparedness ... plays an important role\", followed by a study note (Zhurbinoi, 1978). |\n| claim--sprint-and-distance-training-emphasis-shifts-with-qualification-vankov | FIX | The title says \"Distance Runners Add Anaerobic Work at the Top\". The source (PDF 362, image) says \"distance athletes\", not runners. The entry body rightly calls the sentence \"Unclear as printed\", but the title settles that ambiguity as fact (\"Add\"). Checked and correct: the full Vankov quote, the class-level expansions, the swimmer comparison, the convergence link (labelled Inference), and baseline line 101 (Explicit Q1). `supported` is right. | Change the title to: \"Training Emphasis Shifts With Qualification: Sprinters Move From Aerobic Endurance and Speed to Speed Endurance; for Distance Athletes the Top-Level Emphasis Is Printed as Anaerobic (Vankov 1978)\". Keep the body's unclear-as-printed note as it is. |\n\nCounts: **GO 5, FIX 11, NO-GO 0.**\n\n## Special-attention findings\n\n- **Borrowed management theory.** All three entries name whose model it is: Yerkes and Dodson for the arousal curve, Vroom through Tellier (1978) for expectancy, and Hersey and Blanchard for situational leadership. None presents the general model as the authors' finding. The `untested` ratings are right, since the book gives no evidence for any of them. The one gap is the unsourced introvert/extrovert claim (arousal entry, FIX 2).\n- **Distributed versus concentrated `contested`.** The Issurin critique is stated accurately against the vault brief (p.6 and p.11) and is aimed at the concentrated-unidirectional model. The entry should say that model is Verkhoshansky's. The figure-against-text claim compares dip duration with block length, which are different quantities (FIX).\n- **Block-durations `contested`.** The rating holds: 3-4 weeks (PDF 360), 4-8 weeks (PDF 305) and \"up to 2 months\" (PDF 381) all measure concentrated block or period length. The 4-12 week figures measure something else (stage length at PDF 305 and 381, delayed-effect duration at PDF 375), and so does the 18-22 week figure (the stage needed to exhaust the CAR). The entry mixes these together (FIX).\n- **Fig 6.22.** Graph A decelerates; it is not straight. Two entries assert a contradiction with the text that does not exist, and claim a high-resolution crop check. This is the most serious error in the batch.\n- **Fig 6.23.** Every value the entries use is correct to within about 1 percentage point. The writer's panel A uncertainty is handled honestly. The panel B uncertainty about which curve dips deepest cannot be settled from the image.\n- **Baseline lines 116-117 (football energy systems).** Both are quoted accurately with the correct tags in `three-sport-groups`. Line 116 is quoted accurately in `functional-specialisation`. The source-against-baseline contrast on glycolytic share is presented fairly.\n\n## Missing (up to 5)\n\n1. **Decision-making and problem-solving (PDF 355-356 / p.344-345):** rate each problem high, medium or low on seriousness, urgency and growth; the six problem-solving steps; Maier's (1963) quality-versus-acceptance split and Roskin's four decision styles. This is the most directly usable part of 6.6 for running a department.\n2. **Management versus leadership (PDF 350, 353):** Tellier's management roles (responsibility, authority, accountability) set against leadership as influence (expertise, personality, charisma), and Oncken's four types of authority (position, personality, competence, integrity).\n3. **Blake-Mouton managerial grid and the Tannenbaum-Schmidt continuum with Likert's styles (PDF 353-355, Figs 6.17, 6.18 and 6.20):** the people-versus-production grid coordinates (9,1), (1,9), (1,1), (9,9) and (5,5). This could be one framework entry beside situational leadership.\n4. **Needs theories (PDF 352):** Maslow, Alderfer's ERG and McClelland's achievement, power and affiliation, with the authors' warning that Maslow's hierarchy \"has to be applied with circumspection\", and the transcendence and athletes note (Murphy & White, 1978).\n5. **The endurance monitoring limit (PDF 359):** \"The measures of aerobic and anaerobic output reflect the general level of special work capacity ... but they do not express the internal changes associated with adaptation.\" This is a monitoring point in its own right, and the compensatory entry mentions it only in passing.\n\n## Duplicates and reciprocal links\n\nThere are no duplicates. A grep of the vault and staging for Yerkes, Vroom, Hersey, Farfel, \"fundamental unit\", CAR, distributed loading, Bondarchuk 50% and Vankov found no entry that restates any of these 16. Suggested reciprocal links:\n\n- `framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading...` and staging 6.9.5 `claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes`.\n- `heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks...` and staging 6.9.4 `claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality`, plus vault `heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next` (for the PDF 375 delayed-effect duration).\n- `principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle...` and staging 6.2 `framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap`.\n- `principle--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window` and staging 6.9.4 `principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue`.\n\n## Overall verdict\n\n**GO AFTER FIXES.** Required fixes:\n\n1. Fig 6.22 misread in `four-observations` and `compensatory-adaptation`: graph A decelerates, so remove the figure-against-text claim.\n2. `distributed-vs-concentrated`: replace the Fig 6.23 dip-versus-block comparison; name Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model in the Issurin line; correct \"not for medium-level\".\n3. `concentrated-blocks`: sort the conflict by quantity (block 3-4 / 4-8 weeks / up to 2 months versus stage 4-12 weeks, delayed effect 4-12 weeks, CAR stage 18-22 weeks).\n4. Remove the batch-6a process notes from `mesocycle` and `bondarchuk`.\n5. Label the writer's reasoning as Inference: `mesocycle` Why it works, `functional-specialisation` Chapter 4 pointer, `bondarchuk` Why it works opener, `logical-scheme` \"only route\".\n6. Small source mismatches: `three-sport-groups` \"further subdivide\", `compensatory` \"taken much further\", `vankov` title, `arousal` curve B and the unsourced personality claim, `logical-scheme` shaded boxes, `functional-specialisation` Zhurbinoi placement.\n7. Change the PDF 359 locators from \"text layer only\" to \"checked against the page image\" (I have verified the page).\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11801,"cache_read_input_tokens":203971,"output_tokens":11626,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":216},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":11801},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":11626,"cache_read_input_tokens":203971,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11801,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":11801,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_012FsJxgtq2HPNtG5baZChDK":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch6b_opus.md","content":"# Review: Supertraining Chapter 6, batch 6b (sections 6.4 to 6.8.2)\n\nReviewer: Claude Opus, independent falsification pass, 2026-10-03.\nScope: the 16 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch6b.txt`.\nSource: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, PDF idx 347-362 (printed 336-351), plus PDF 305 and 381.\n\nMethod: I extracted the text layer for PDF 305, 347-362 and 381. I rendered and opened at 200 dpi PDF 348, 349, 351, 353, 354, 357, 358, 359, 360, 361 and 362, and the middle of 381. I also cropped Fig 6.22 and both panels of Fig 6.23 at 400 dpi and read values with pixel measurements (Fig 6.23 A: 11.12 px per 1%; B: 11.13 px per 1%; 64.3 to 64.6 px per week). I checked all 19 cited `method-baseline.md` lines (27, 30, 32, 59, 64, 75, 101, 105, 106, 115, 116, 117, 123, 151, 153, 171, 183, 185, 204). Every quote and tag matches. All 62 wikilink targets resolve. Each file has exactly six em dashes, all in the section headers, and no wikilink is wrapped across lines. Renders are in `staging/_renders/r6b_*.png`.\n\n**PDF 359:** The writer read this page from the text layer only. I opened the page image, and the text layer matches it word for word for every quote used. (The adaptation definition, \"adaptation cannot be considered an equilibrium process ...\", is italic in the original.) The fixer can change \"text layer only\" to \"checked against the page image\" in the four entries that cite PDF 359.\n\n**PDF 381:** I checked the image. The text says \"prolonged stages (4-12 weeks)\" for unidirectional training and \"up to 2 months\" for concentrated loading.\n\n## Entries\n\n| File | Verdict | Problem (entry vs source, with page) | Exact correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| framework--logical-scheme-of-training-management-load-fitness-state-external-interactions | FIX | (1) The entry says \"Athlete's fitness state (shaded box A) ... Athlete's external interactions (shaded box B)\". The Fig 6.12 image (PDF 348 / p.337) shows box A as the whole shaded band {Training Load, Training Potential of Loading, Athlete's Fitness State}. Box B is the larger shaded region {Athlete's Fitness State, Training Effect of Loading, Athlete's External Interactions}, with fitness state in both. The text's subsystem list is right; only the figure walk-through mislabels the boxes. (2) Why it works says \"fitness state is the only route from load to result, so both ... links must be measured\" and presents this as the authors' reason. PDF 347-348 gives the one-way chain and the two control levels, but neither \"only route\" nor \"must be measured\" is stated. Checked and correct: all the 6.4 quotes (PDF 347-348), the Fig 6.12 box names and the arrow from Sports Competition and Laboratory Tests into Assessment of Effect, the \"lower the measures for the first one (A)\" quote, and baseline lines 64 and 32. | (1) Replace the parentheticals with: \"the shaded band A (Training Load, Training Potential of Loading, Athlete's Fitness State) and the shaded region B (Athlete's Fitness State, Training Effect of Loading, Athlete's External Interactions), which overlap at fitness state\". (2) Change the Why-it-works lead to: \"The authors set out a one-way chain (load, fitness state, external interactions) and control at two levels (PDF 348). Inference: fitness state is therefore the only route from load to result, so both links need measuring.\" |\n| heuristic--build-training-theory-from-interacting-complexes-and-coach-scientist-cooperation | GO | Checked: the \"individual fragments of research\" opening, the \"All too often ... single factor\", \"very rarely\" and specialist quotes (PDF 349 / p.338), \"cannot be condemned ... 'their' types of sport\", the coach-scientist passage and the VO2max fall in the competition period (PDF 350 / p.339), and every Fig 6.13 box against the image (PDF 349). The linked VO2max claim does carry the competition-period fall (PDF 259-260). Baseline lines 27 and 30 (Explicit) match. The When NOT section is substantive. `untested` is right. | None. |\n| heuristic--arousal-optimum-is-individual-so-fit-restoration-to-personality-and-task-complexity | FIX | (1) Fig 6.15 description: the entry says \"a narrow spike A at low arousal, and spikes B and C near high arousal.\" In the image (PDF 351 / p.340), B is not a separate spike. It is a second hump on the right-hand descending limb of the broad inverted-U, which matches the text's \"'second wind' phenomenon (curve B)\". Only A and C are separate narrow spikes. (2) Borrowed models: the entry correctly names Yerkes and Dodson as the source of the curve. The introvert/extrovert arousal claim in point 2 is also a borrowed idea, but the book names no source for it, and the entry lists it under \"The authors' points\" with nothing to say it is not their own finding. Checked and correct: every other quote on PDF 351 and the PDF 352 carry-over (\"overstress\", \"supercompensation\"), the \"(Siff, 1990)\" note on PDF 350, baseline line 153, the Example's golf, gymnastics and pole vaulting, and `untested`. | (1) Replace the Fig 6.15 sentence with: \"one broad inverted-U over 'low / optimal / high' arousal; a narrow spike A at low arousal; a second hump B on the right-hand slope of the broad curve ('second wind'); and a narrow spike C at high arousal.\" (2) In point 2, add: \"The book gives no source for the personality difference; it is presented as a general claim ('appears'), not as the authors' finding.\" Optional provenance notes (Eysenck for the arousal and personality idea, the 1908 Yerkes-Dodson task) need a checked citation before they are added. |\n| heuristic--expectancy-motivation-break-distant-goals-into-attainable-steps | GO | Checked against the PDF 353 / p.342 image: the \"Vroom's Expectancy Theory of Motivation\" naming, \"(Tellier, 1978)\", the V and B definitions, F = V x B, the -5 to +5 and 0 to 1 scales, the sum over outcomes, \"Detailed examples ... outside the scope of this book\", and the leader quote. The model is named as Vroom's, and the scales are reported as given, not as the authors' finding. The entry flags the single-product versus sum wording correctly. Baseline line 171 (Inferred) matches. Example is labelled Inference. `untested` is right. | None. Optional: say that the two-factor form and the scales are as reported through Tellier, so later readers do not cite them as Vroom's original formulation. |\n| framework--situational-leadership-coach-matches-style-to-athlete-maturity | GO | Checked against the Fig 6.19 image (PDF 354 / p.343). Relationship behaviour is on the vertical axis and task behaviour on the horizontal. The quadrants are S3 Participating (top left), S2 Selling (top right), S4 Delegating (bottom left) and S1 Telling (bottom right). There is a bell curve, and the maturity strip runs M4 to M1 from left to right, so M1 pairs with S1. The quotes (\"ought to be implemented according to each situation\", the maturity list, \"low, moderate or high (M1, M2, M3 or M4)\", \"hence the shape of the curve\") all match. The model is credited to Hersey and Blanchard, and the three-words-for-four-codes gap is flagged. Baseline line 204 matches. `untested` is right. | None. Minor: the Why-it-works clause \"a follower low in experience ... needs direction\" is the model's logic read off the figure; prefixing \"Inference:\" would be cleaner. |\n| framework--three-sport-groups-acyclic-cyclic-complex-for-programming | FIX | When NOT says \"the authors' own call to 'further subdivide' applies\" to positions within the complex group. On PDF 357 / p.346 (image) the call (\"One should further subdivide the sports in each of these broad groups\") comes after the acyclic and cyclic bullets and before \"Finally, one should identify a third group\". It is made for the two broad groups only, and the complex group is never subdivided. Checked and correct: all the 6.7 quotes (PDF 356-357), including \"the greater part being derived from glycolytic reactions\" (in the third-group paragraph); Farfel (1969); the 100 m example; baseline lines 115, 116 and 117, with quotes and tags matching. The source-against-baseline contrast on football is fairly stated. | Change the sentence to: \"Inference: within the complex group, positions differ enough ... that the group label decides little on its own. The authors call for subdivision only within the acyclic and cyclic groups (PDF 357); extending it to the complex group is this entry's inference.\" |\n| principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-the-unit-and-planned-dips-are-not-errors | FIX | (1) Why it works: \"The authors' reason is that the effects they care about ... unfold over weeks, so judging each microcycle against the previous one misreads them.\" PDF 358 / p.347 gives \"modern sporting requirements\" and \"common sense\" as the basis and calls for \"An extensive study of ... long-term adaptation\". The weeks-long-effects mechanism is the writer's. (2) When NOT contains process notes: \"Batch 6a (sections 6.1 to 6.3 ...) may carry an overlapping entry ...\". The brief forbids review or process history in entries. (3) Missing tension: the 6.2 entry `framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap` quotes the book calling the training session \"the fundamental unit\" (PDF 327 / p.316), while 6.8.1 says the fundamental unit \"should be the mesocycle\". Checked and correct: the three bulleted recommendations, the Folbrot and Weigert citations and the verdict quote (PDF 358 image), and baseline lines 59 and 106. | (1) Rewrite as: \"The authors' stated basis is 'modern sporting requirements' and 'common sense' (PDF 358); they give no study. Inference: the effects they go on to describe (long-term adaptation, the delayed rise after concentrated loading) unfold over weeks, so microcycle-to-microcycle comparison would misread them.\" (2) Delete the batch 6a sentence. Replace it with: \"The book calls the training session 'the fundamental unit' of periodisation in 6.2 (PDF 327 / p.316) and the mesocycle the fundamental unit of training in 6.8.1; the first is a structural unit, the second a planning unit. Inference: the book does not reconcile the wording.\" Link [[framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap]]. |\n| claim--four-observations-on-long-term-speed-strength-adaptation | FIX | **Figure misread under a false verification claim.** The entry says it checked Fig 6.22 \"against the page image and a high-resolution crop\" and that \"in the image A is close to a straight line over the three years drawn, with no visible peak\". It treats this as figure-against-text. My 400 dpi crop of PDF 358 / p.347 shows graph A's rise shrinking each year: about 220 px in Year 1, 145 px in Year 2 and 65 px in Year 3 (slopes of roughly 0.39, 0.25 and 0.11). That is a decelerating curve, consistent with the text's \"parabola whose peak is eventually reached with time\" (PDF 359). No peak is reached within the three years drawn, which the text does not claim either. The contradiction does not exist. Checked and correct: all four conclusions word for word (PDF 358 image), the B and C assignment to bi-cyclic and mono-cyclic, the A and D descriptions, the endurance caveat (PDF 359, now image-checked), and baseline lines 64 and 185. `supported` is right. | Replace \"a thick, nearly straight rising line A\" with \"a thick rising line A whose slope shrinks each year (steepest in Year 1, flattest in Year 3)\". Delete the \"Figure against text\" sentences in When NOT. Replace them with: \"Graph A flattens year by year, in line with the text's parabola (PDF 359); no peak is drawn within the three years.\" Change the PDF 359 locators from \"text layer only\" to \"checked against the page image\". |\n| claim--compensatory-adaptation-is-temporary-and-precedes-stable-adaptation | FIX | (1) The same Fig 6.22 misread: When NOT says \"Fig 6.22's graph A looks nearly straight over the three years drawn, not parabolic\". As shown above, A decelerates and matches the parabola. (2) Why it works says endurance adaptation \"has to be taken much further\". On PDF 359 that phrase is about research, not adaptation: \"The research done by Folbrot (1941), Selye ... and others has to be taken much further in its application to ... especially endurance sports.\" (3) \"Page 359 was read from the text layer only\": now checked against the image, and every quote matches. Checked and correct: the compensatory and pre-adaptation quotes, Kuznyetsov 1979, the Bauer and Bernstein definition, the heterochronicity bullets, \"parabola\", \"genetically predetermined\" and CAR (all PDF 359 image). The genetic-limit inference is labelled. | (1) Delete \"and Fig 6.22's graph A looks nearly straight over the three years drawn, not parabolic\". Optionally add: \"Graph A's slope shrinks each year, consistent with the parabola.\" (2) Change to: \"... and that research on adaptation (Folbrot, Selye) 'has to be taken much further' for endurance sports; until then they assume 'the same, above-mentioned chief mechanisms ...'\". (3) Change the locator to \"checked against the page image\" and delete the \"Page 359 was read from the text layer only\" sentence. |\n| principle--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window | GO | Checked: the CAR quotes on PDF 359 (image) and PDF 360 (image), Letunov 1952, Prokor 1959 (the book's spelling), the re-adaptation definition at the top of PDF 360, Matveyev 1964, and Beckman 1961 and Yakovlev 1977. The Chapter 1 two-definitions point is confirmed in the vault `claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation`. Baseline line 75 matches (Explicit Q2, Q2a). `supported` is right. When NOT is substantive. | None required. Minor: the PDF 359 locator can now read \"checked against the page image\". The Bompa entry describes a *standard* load leading to a plateau, not a below-threshold one, so \"(below threshold, plateau ...)\" should read \"(an unchanging load, plateau; an excessive one, maladaptation)\". |\n| framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation | FIX | **Fig 6.23 values: all verified.** Panel A ends at about 144, 128, 114, 108 and 105%. A hump peaks at about 113% near week 8.8, then falls to the lowest end value; by end position and label order (maximum strength, absolute strength, speed) that is speed, and the entry's \"by that reading\" caveat is fair. Panel B lows run from about 83% to 96% at weeks 4-5. Curves cross back above 100% at about weeks 7-9 and end at about 157, 142-143, 129 (peak of about 130-131 near week 16.5), 120 and 114%. Dashed lines sit at weeks 6 and 12. Which curve dips deepest cannot be settled because the curves cross at weeks 7-9, as the entry says. **Problem 1:** When NOT has \"Figure against text: the figure's depressed phase (below 100% until about weeks 7-9) runs longer than the 3-4 week concentrated block\". This compares dip duration with block length, which are different quantities. The text on the same page says the concentrated volume sits \"in the first half of the training phase\" (PDF 360 image), and a dip through weeks 7-9 of 18 is that first half. Indicators are expected to stay low until volume drops. **Problem 2:** The Issurin (2016) critique is stated accurately: insufficient evidence for broad use in combat, team-sport and swimming preparation, with fitness gains but no reported effect on team practice or competition (p.6), and better suited to narrow target-quality sports (p.11), all as in the vault brief. But the entry does not say whose model is being criticised; per the brief, it is Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model, not Issurin's A-T-R. **Problem 3:** \"Not for novices or medium-level athletes, by the authors' own scope\". PDF 360 says the method \"was devised for highly qualified athletes\"; it does not forbid it for medium-level athletes. And 6.9.5 (`claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes`, PDF 371) says \"both methods are successful in training athletes of medium qualification\". Checked and correct: all the PDF 360-361 quotes, Komarova 1984 (\"he errs\"), and baseline line 105 (Inferred, \"Not claimed in the interview\"). `contested` stands, for the Issurin reason. | (1) Replace the Fig 6.23 \"figure against text\" sentence with: \"Panel B's depression lasts until about weeks 7-9 of 18, which fits the text's 'concentrated in the first half of the training phase' (PDF 360). The figure marks 6-week intervals (dashed lines at weeks 6 and 12) and shows no 3-4 week blocks or 7-10 day pauses, so it does not illustrate the block durations given on the same page. Its x-axis ends at 18 weeks; the text's period is 18-22 weeks.\" (2) Change \"Issurin (2016) judged the concentrated-unidirectional evidence ...\" to \"Issurin (2016) judged the evidence for Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model ...\". (3) Replace the last sentence with: \"The authors devised it for highly qualified athletes (PDF 360); in 6.9.5 they say both forms work at medium qualification (PDF 371), so it is not required there.\" Add the reciprocal link [[claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes]]. |\n| heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery | FIX | **`contested` is justified, but the conflict mixes up different quantities.** Sorted by what each number measures: (a) **Concentrated block or period length:** 3-4 weeks (PDF 360 / p.349, image); \"4-8 week concentrated blocks\" (PDF 305 / p.294); \"a limited period (of up to 2 months)\" (PDF 381 / p.370, image). These are the same quantity, so the conflict is real. (b) **Unidirectional training stage length:** \"unidirectional training over periods as long as 4-12 weeks\" (PDF 305) and \"prolonged stages (4-12 weeks)\" (PDF 381). This is a stage of one emphasis, not a concentrated block. (c) **Delayed-effect duration:** 4-12 weeks (PDF 375, recorded in the vault `heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next`). (d) **Latency to gains:** 1-3 weeks (PDF 305). (e) **Stage to exhaust the CAR:** 18-22 weeks (PDF 360). (f) **Upper limit for one primary emphasis:** months, by quality (staging 6.9.4 `claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality`, PDF 370-373). The entry files the 4-12 week figure under \"Contested inside the book (concentrated block duration)\", next to the 4-8 weeks, as if both were block lengths. Checked and correct: every numbered quote (PDF 360 image), Zhikharevich 1976 and Sirenko 1980, the arithmetic (17-21 and 11-15 weeks), and baseline line 183. The Issurin (six weeks) and Bondarchuk (four-week blocks) Related notes match the vault files. | Rewrite the When NOT conflict paragraph as: \"**Contested inside the book (concentrated block length).** 3-4 weeks here (PDF 360 / p.349); '4-8 week concentrated blocks' in 5.3.3 (PDF 305 / p.294); 'a limited period (of up to 2 months)' in 6.12.2 (PDF 381 / p.370). **Different quantities, not part of the conflict:** 4-12 weeks is the length of a unidirectional training *stage* (PDF 305, 381); 4-12 weeks is also how long the delayed training effect lasts (PDF 375, see [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]]); 1-3 weeks is the latency to gains (PDF 305); 18-22 weeks is the stage needed to exhaust the CAR (PDF 360).\" Keep the \"three 3-4 week blocks give about 9-12 weeks\" reconciliation, labelled Inference, as a link between block length (a) and stage length (b). Add as Inference: \"5.3.3 and 6.12.2 both define loading as concentrated by one month's volume (23-25% of the annual volume, PDF 305, 381), which makes the month the working unit; 'up to 2 months' reads as a ceiling.\" Add the link [[claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality]]. |\n| heuristic--restorative-stage-length-scales-with-homeostatic-disturbance | GO | Checked: the full restorative-stage quote against the PDF 361 / p.350 image, the \"after the volume of loading is decreased\" quote (PDF 360), the 1-3 week latency after 4-8 week blocks (PDF 305), and the 7-10 day pause (PDF 360). The Bompa & Haff quote matches the vault `allow-restoration` entry word for word. Baseline line 151 (Inferred) matches. The GPP mechanism is correctly marked as not given. The Example is labelled Inference. `supported` is right. | None. Minor: \"as quoted in the 5.3.3 entry above\" should read \"as quoted in [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]]\", since \"above\" means nothing in a standalone note. |\n| claim--bondarchuk-50-percent-more-volume-did-not-prolong-training-effect | FIX | (1) Process note in the entry body: \"The Table 6.1 and 6.2.2 material it points to is in batch 6a's pages (PDF pp. 334-336), not checked here.\" The brief forbids review or process history. (I confirmed that 6.2.2 starts on PDF 334 and Table 6.1 is referenced on PDF 335.) (2) Why it works opens \"Bondarchuk's study as reported does not test intensity at all; the comparison with intensity is the authors' reading\". That is a correct observation, but it is the writer's, presented without a label. Checked and correct: the full quote, including the italic \"the importance of paying careful attention to zones of loading intensity\" (PDF 361 image), and baseline line 105 (Inferred, \"Not claimed in the interview\"). The tension with the PDF 360 \"more extensive ... the longer the new functional level is maintained\" claim is accurate and labelled. `untested` is acceptable: one group, reported second-hand, with Bondarchuk's own \"strictly\" caveat. | (1) Replace the batch sentence with: \"The 6.2.2 intensity-zone material and Table 6.1 it points to are at PDF pp. 334-336 / printed pp. 323-325.\" (2) Prefix \"Inference:\" to the first sentence of Why it works. |\n| principle--functional-specialisation-is-a-primary-emphasis-not-an-exclusive-energy-system | FIX | (1) Why it works: \"For the cyclic side they point to Chapter 4's argument that endurance rests on muscular as well as respiratory qualities.\" PDF 362 / p.351 (image) points only to 2.2.3 and 5.4.3, and supports the cyclic point with Zhurbinoi (1978). There is no Chapter 4 pointer on the page. (2) \"(Zhurbinoi, 1978)\" is attached to \"strength preparedness, mainly in the form of strength-endurance, plays an important role\". In the source, the citation closes the *next* sentence (\"Research shows that, with a relatively moderate increase in muscular strength ... (Zhurbinoi, 1978)\"). Checked and correct: the 6.8.2 quotes (PDF 361-362 images), the \"classification scheme of 6.5\" note (a correct catch: the scheme is in 6.7), Sargent 1926, Henry 1951, Margaria 1963, and the triple jump example. Baseline line 123 (Inferred) and line 116 (Goals 1 and 2 are alactic and aerobic) match. | (1) Rewrite as: \"For the cyclic side the authors cite Zhurbinoi (1978) on strength-endurance (PDF 362). Inference: Chapter 4's argument that endurance rests on muscular as well as respiratory qualities (see Related) supports the same point.\" (2) Quote both sentences with the citation where the source puts it, or write: \"strength preparedness ... plays an important role\", followed by a study note (Zhurbinoi, 1978). |\n| claim--sprint-and-distance-training-emphasis-shifts-with-qualification-vankov | FIX | The title says \"Distance Runners Add Anaerobic Work at the Top\". The source (PDF 362, image) says \"distance athletes\", not runners. The entry body rightly calls the sentence \"Unclear as printed\", but the title settles that ambiguity as fact (\"Add\"). Checked and correct: the full Vankov quote, the class-level expansions, the swimmer comparison, the convergence link (labelled Inference), and baseline line 101 (Explicit Q1). `supported` is right. | Change the title to: \"Training Emphasis Shifts With Qualification: Sprinters Move From Aerobic Endurance and Speed to Speed Endurance; for Distance Athletes the Top-Level Emphasis Is Printed as Anaerobic (Vankov 1978)\". Keep the body's unclear-as-printed note as it is. |\n\nCounts: **GO 5, FIX 11, NO-GO 0.**\n\n## Special-attention findings\n\n- **Borrowed management theory.** All three entries name whose model it is: Yerkes and Dodson for the arousal curve, Vroom through Tellier (1978) for expectancy, and Hersey and Blanchard for situational leadership. None presents the general model as the authors' finding. The `untested` ratings are right, since the book gives no evidence for any of them. The one gap is the unsourced introvert/extrovert claim (arousal entry, FIX 2).\n- **Distributed versus concentrated `contested`.** The Issurin critique is stated accurately against the vault brief (p.6 and p.11) and is aimed at the concentrated-unidirectional model. The entry should say that model is Verkhoshansky's. The figure-against-text claim compares dip duration with block length, which are different quantities (FIX).\n- **Block-durations `contested`.** The rating holds: 3-4 weeks (PDF 360), 4-8 weeks (PDF 305) and \"up to 2 months\" (PDF 381) all measure concentrated block or period length. The 4-12 week figures measure something else (stage length at PDF 305 and 381, delayed-effect duration at PDF 375), and so does the 18-22 week figure (the stage needed to exhaust the CAR). The entry mixes these together (FIX).\n- **Fig 6.22.** Graph A decelerates; it is not straight. Two entries assert a contradiction with the text that does not exist, and claim a high-resolution crop check. This is the most serious error in the batch.\n- **Fig 6.23.** Every value the entries use is correct to within about 1 percentage point. The writer's panel A uncertainty is handled honestly. The panel B uncertainty about which curve dips deepest cannot be settled from the image.\n- **Baseline lines 116-117 (football energy systems).** Both are quoted accurately with the correct tags in `three-sport-groups`. Line 116 is quoted accurately in `functional-specialisation`. The source-against-baseline contrast on glycolytic share is presented fairly.\n\n## Missing (up to 5)\n\n1. **Decision-making and problem-solving (PDF 355-356 / p.344-345):** rate each problem high, medium or low on seriousness, urgency and growth; the six problem-solving steps; Maier's (1963) quality-versus-acceptance split and Roskin's four decision styles. This is the most directly usable part of 6.6 for running a department.\n2. **Management versus leadership (PDF 350, 353):** Tellier's management roles (responsibility, authority, accountability) set against leadership as influence (expertise, personality, charisma), and Oncken's four types of authority (position, personality, competence, integrity).\n3. **Blake-Mouton managerial grid and the Tannenbaum-Schmidt continuum with Likert's styles (PDF 353-355, Figs 6.17, 6.18 and 6.20):** the people-versus-production grid coordinates (9,1), (1,9), (1,1), (9,9) and (5,5). This could be one framework entry beside situational leadership.\n4. **Needs theories (PDF 352):** Maslow, Alderfer's ERG and McClelland's achievement, power and affiliation, with the authors' warning that Maslow's hierarchy \"has to be applied with circumspection\", and the transcendence and athletes note (Murphy & White, 1978).\n5. **The endurance monitoring limit (PDF 359):** \"The measures of aerobic and anaerobic output reflect the general level of special work capacity ... but they do not express the internal changes associated with adaptation.\" This is a monitoring point in its own right, and the compensatory entry mentions it only in passing.\n\n## Duplicates and reciprocal links\n\nThere are no duplicates. A grep of the vault and staging for Yerkes, Vroom, Hersey, Farfel, \"fundamental unit\", CAR, distributed loading, Bondarchuk 50% and Vankov found no entry that restates any of these 16. Suggested reciprocal links:\n\n- `framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading...` and staging 6.9.5 `claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes`.\n- `heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks...` and staging 6.9.4 `claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality`, plus vault `heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next` (for the PDF 375 delayed-effect duration).\n- `principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle...` and staging 6.2 `framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap`.\n- `principle--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window` and staging 6.9.4 `principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue`.\n\n## Overall verdict\n\n**GO AFTER FIXES.** Required fixes:\n\n1. Fig 6.22 misread in `four-observations` and `compensatory-adaptation`: graph A decelerates, so remove the figure-against-text claim.\n2. `distributed-vs-concentrated`: replace the Fig 6.23 dip-versus-block comparison; name Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model in the Issurin line; correct \"not for medium-level\".\n3. `concentrated-blocks`: sort the conflict by quantity (block 3-4 / 4-8 weeks / up to 2 months versus stage 4-12 weeks, delayed effect 4-12 weeks, CAR stage 18-22 weeks).\n4. Remove the batch-6a process notes from `mesocycle` and `bondarchuk`.\n5. Label the writer's reasoning as Inference: `mesocycle` Why it works, `functional-specialisation` Chapter 4 pointer, `bondarchuk` Why it works opener, `logical-scheme` \"only route\".\n6. Small source mismatches: `three-sport-groups` \"further subdivide\", `compensatory` \"taken much further\", `vankov` title, `arousal` curve B and the unsourced personality claim, `logical-scheme` shaded boxes, `functional-specialisation` Zhurbinoi placement.\n7. Change the PDF 359 locators from \"text layer only\" to \"checked against the page image\" (I have verified the page).\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffnZ5mPVB2QnXeoD9ofo","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"76359b7b-01d3-4e8b-b611-2a69d77a1ddb","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:37:04.517Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"76359b7b-01d3-4e8b-b611-2a69d77a1ddb","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f4ad0537-a077-4262-99a8-3002dada006a","agentId":"aeede644703c0347a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012FsJxgtq2HPNtG5baZChDK","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch6b_opus.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"f43af58b-c00d-435b-9992-e27b2f9eb7ea","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:37:04.640Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch6b_opus.md","content":"# Review: Supertraining Chapter 6, batch 6b (sections 6.4 to 6.8.2)\n\nReviewer: Claude Opus, independent falsification pass, 2026-10-03.\nScope: the 16 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch6b.txt`.\nSource: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, PDF idx 347-362 (printed 336-351), plus PDF 305 and 381.\n\nMethod: I extracted the text layer for PDF 305, 347-362 and 381. I rendered and opened at 200 dpi PDF 348, 349, 351, 353, 354, 357, 358, 359, 360, 361 and 362, and the middle of 381. I also cropped Fig 6.22 and both panels of Fig 6.23 at 400 dpi and read values with pixel measurements (Fig 6.23 A: 11.12 px per 1%; B: 11.13 px per 1%; 64.3 to 64.6 px per week). I checked all 19 cited `method-baseline.md` lines (27, 30, 32, 59, 64, 75, 101, 105, 106, 115, 116, 117, 123, 151, 153, 171, 183, 185, 204). Every quote and tag matches. All 62 wikilink targets resolve. Each file has exactly six em dashes, all in the section headers, and no wikilink is wrapped across lines. Renders are in `staging/_renders/r6b_*.png`.\n\n**PDF 359:** The writer read this page from the text layer only. I opened the page image, and the text layer matches it word for word for every quote used. (The adaptation definition, \"adaptation cannot be considered an equilibrium process ...\", is italic in the original.) The fixer can change \"text layer only\" to \"checked against the page image\" in the four entries that cite PDF 359.\n\n**PDF 381:** I checked the image. The text says \"prolonged stages (4-12 weeks)\" for unidirectional training and \"up to 2 months\" for concentrated loading.\n\n## Entries\n\n| File | Verdict | Problem (entry vs source, with page) | Exact correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| framework--logical-scheme-of-training-management-load-fitness-state-external-interactions | FIX | (1) The entry says \"Athlete's fitness state (shaded box A) ... Athlete's external interactions (shaded box B)\". The Fig 6.12 image (PDF 348 / p.337) shows box A as the whole shaded band {Training Load, Training Potential of Loading, Athlete's Fitness State}. Box B is the larger shaded region {Athlete's Fitness State, Training Effect of Loading, Athlete's External Interactions}, with fitness state in both. The text's subsystem list is right; only the figure walk-through mislabels the boxes. (2) Why it works says \"fitness state is the only route from load to result, so both ... links must be measured\" and presents this as the authors' reason. PDF 347-348 gives the one-way chain and the two control levels, but neither \"only route\" nor \"must be measured\" is stated. Checked and correct: all the 6.4 quotes (PDF 347-348), the Fig 6.12 box names and the arrow from Sports Competition and Laboratory Tests into Assessment of Effect, the \"lower the measures for the first one (A)\" quote, and baseline lines 64 and 32. | (1) Replace the parentheticals with: \"the shaded band A (Training Load, Training Potential of Loading, Athlete's Fitness State) and the shaded region B (Athlete's Fitness State, Training Effect of Loading, Athlete's External Interactions), which overlap at fitness state\". (2) Change the Why-it-works lead to: \"The authors set out a one-way chain (load, fitness state, external interactions) and control at two levels (PDF 348). Inference: fitness state is therefore the only route from load to result, so both links need measuring.\" |\n| heuristic--build-training-theory-from-interacting-complexes-and-coach-scientist-cooperation | GO | Checked: the \"individual fragments of research\" opening, the \"All too often ... single factor\", \"very rarely\" and specialist quotes (PDF 349 / p.338), \"cannot be condemned ... 'their' types of sport\", the coach-scientist passage and the VO2max fall in the competition period (PDF 350 / p.339), and every Fig 6.13 box against the image (PDF 349). The linked VO2max claim does carry the competition-period fall (PDF 259-260). Baseline lines 27 and 30 (Explicit) match. The When NOT section is substantive. `untested` is right. | None. |\n| heuristic--arousal-optimum-is-individual-so-fit-restoration-to-personality-and-task-complexity | FIX | (1) Fig 6.15 description: the entry says \"a narrow spike A at low arousal, and spikes B and C near high arousal.\" In the image (PDF 351 / p.340), B is not a separate spike. It is a second hump on the right-hand descending limb of the broad inverted-U, which matches the text's \"'second wind' phenomenon (curve B)\". Only A and C are separate narrow spikes. (2) Borrowed models: the entry correctly names Yerkes and Dodson as the source of the curve. The introvert/extrovert arousal claim in point 2 is also a borrowed idea, but the book names no source for it, and the entry lists it under \"The authors' points\" with nothing to say it is not their own finding. Checked and correct: every other quote on PDF 351 and the PDF 352 carry-over (\"overstress\", \"supercompensation\"), the \"(Siff, 1990)\" note on PDF 350, baseline line 153, the Example's golf, gymnastics and pole vaulting, and `untested`. | (1) Replace the Fig 6.15 sentence with: \"one broad inverted-U over 'low / optimal / high' arousal; a narrow spike A at low arousal; a second hump B on the right-hand slope of the broad curve ('second wind'); and a narrow spike C at high arousal.\" (2) In point 2, add: \"The book gives no source for the personality difference; it is presented as a general claim ('appears'), not as the authors' finding.\" Optional provenance notes (Eysenck for the arousal and personality idea, the 1908 Yerkes-Dodson task) need a checked citation before they are added. |\n| heuristic--expectancy-motivation-break-distant-goals-into-attainable-steps | GO | Checked against the PDF 353 / p.342 image: the \"Vroom's Expectancy Theory of Motivation\" naming, \"(Tellier, 1978)\", the V and B definitions, F = V x B, the -5 to +5 and 0 to 1 scales, the sum over outcomes, \"Detailed examples ... outside the scope of this book\", and the leader quote. The model is named as Vroom's, and the scales are reported as given, not as the authors' finding. The entry flags the single-product versus sum wording correctly. Baseline line 171 (Inferred) matches. Example is labelled Inference. `untested` is right. | None. Optional: say that the two-factor form and the scales are as reported through Tellier, so later readers do not cite them as Vroom's original formulation. |\n| framework--situational-leadership-coach-matches-style-to-athlete-maturity | GO | Checked against the Fig 6.19 image (PDF 354 / p.343). Relationship behaviour is on the vertical axis and task behaviour on the horizontal. The quadrants are S3 Participating (top left), S2 Selling (top right), S4 Delegating (bottom left) and S1 Telling (bottom right). There is a bell curve, and the maturity strip runs M4 to M1 from left to right, so M1 pairs with S1. The quotes (\"ought to be implemented according to each situation\", the maturity list, \"low, moderate or high (M1, M2, M3 or M4)\", \"hence the shape of the curve\") all match. The model is credited to Hersey and Blanchard, and the three-words-for-four-codes gap is flagged. Baseline line 204 matches. `untested` is right. | None. Minor: the Why-it-works clause \"a follower low in experience ... needs direction\" is the model's logic read off the figure; prefixing \"Inference:\" would be cleaner. |\n| framework--three-sport-groups-acyclic-cyclic-complex-for-programming | FIX | When NOT says \"the authors' own call to 'further subdivide' applies\" to positions within the complex group. On PDF 357 / p.346 (image) the call (\"One should further subdivide the sports in each of these broad groups\") comes after the acyclic and cyclic bullets and before \"Finally, one should identify a third group\". It is made for the two broad groups only, and the complex group is never subdivided. Checked and correct: all the 6.7 quotes (PDF 356-357), including \"the greater part being derived from glycolytic reactions\" (in the third-group paragraph); Farfel (1969); the 100 m example; baseline lines 115, 116 and 117, with quotes and tags matching. The source-against-baseline contrast on football is fairly stated. | Change the sentence to: \"Inference: within the complex group, positions differ enough ... that the group label decides little on its own. The authors call for subdivision only within the acyclic and cyclic groups (PDF 357); extending it to the complex group is this entry's inference.\" |\n| principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-the-unit-and-planned-dips-are-not-errors | FIX | (1) Why it works: \"The authors' reason is that the effects they care about ... unfold over weeks, so judging each microcycle against the previous one misreads them.\" PDF 358 / p.347 gives \"modern sporting requirements\" and \"common sense\" as the basis and calls for \"An extensive study of ... long-term adaptation\". The weeks-long-effects mechanism is the writer's. (2) When NOT contains process notes: \"Batch 6a (sections 6.1 to 6.3 ...) may carry an overlapping entry ...\". The brief forbids review or process history in entries. (3) Missing tension: the 6.2 entry `framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap` quotes the book calling the training session \"the fundamental unit\" (PDF 327 / p.316), while 6.8.1 says the fundamental unit \"should be the mesocycle\". Checked and correct: the three bulleted recommendations, the Folbrot and Weigert citations and the verdict quote (PDF 358 image), and baseline lines 59 and 106. | (1) Rewrite as: \"The authors' stated basis is 'modern sporting requirements' and 'common sense' (PDF 358); they give no study. Inference: the effects they go on to describe (long-term adaptation, the delayed rise after concentrated loading) unfold over weeks, so microcycle-to-microcycle comparison would misread them.\" (2) Delete the batch 6a sentence. Replace it with: \"The book calls the training session 'the fundamental unit' of periodisation in 6.2 (PDF 327 / p.316) and the mesocycle the fundamental unit of training in 6.8.1; the first is a structural unit, the second a planning unit. Inference: the book does not reconcile the wording.\" Link [[framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap]]. |\n| claim--four-observations-on-long-term-speed-strength-adaptation | FIX | **Figure misread under a false verification claim.** The entry says it checked Fig 6.22 \"against the page image and a high-resolution crop\" and that \"in the image A is close to a straight line over the three years drawn, with no visible peak\". It treats this as figure-against-text. My 400 dpi crop of PDF 358 / p.347 shows graph A's rise shrinking each year: about 220 px in Year 1, 145 px in Year 2 and 65 px in Year 3 (slopes of roughly 0.39, 0.25 and 0.11). That is a decelerating curve, consistent with the text's \"parabola whose peak is eventually reached with time\" (PDF 359). No peak is reached within the three years drawn, which the text does not claim either. The contradiction does not exist. Checked and correct: all four conclusions word for word (PDF 358 image), the B and C assignment to bi-cyclic and mono-cyclic, the A and D descriptions, the endurance caveat (PDF 359, now image-checked), and baseline lines 64 and 185. `supported` is right. | Replace \"a thick, nearly straight rising line A\" with \"a thick rising line A whose slope shrinks each year (steepest in Year 1, flattest in Year 3)\". Delete the \"Figure against text\" sentences in When NOT. Replace them with: \"Graph A flattens year by year, in line with the text's parabola (PDF 359); no peak is drawn within the three years.\" Change the PDF 359 locators from \"text layer only\" to \"checked against the page image\". |\n| claim--compensatory-adaptation-is-temporary-and-precedes-stable-adaptation | FIX | (1) The same Fig 6.22 misread: When NOT says \"Fig 6.22's graph A looks nearly straight over the three years drawn, not parabolic\". As shown above, A decelerates and matches the parabola. (2) Why it works says endurance adaptation \"has to be taken much further\". On PDF 359 that phrase is about research, not adaptation: \"The research done by Folbrot (1941), Selye ... and others has to be taken much further in its application to ... especially endurance sports.\" (3) \"Page 359 was read from the text layer only\": now checked against the image, and every quote matches. Checked and correct: the compensatory and pre-adaptation quotes, Kuznyetsov 1979, the Bauer and Bernstein definition, the heterochronicity bullets, \"parabola\", \"genetically predetermined\" and CAR (all PDF 359 image). The genetic-limit inference is labelled. | (1) Delete \"and Fig 6.22's graph A looks nearly straight over the three years drawn, not parabolic\". Optionally add: \"Graph A's slope shrinks each year, consistent with the parabola.\" (2) Change to: \"... and that research on adaptation (Folbrot, Selye) 'has to be taken much further' for endurance sports; until then they assume 'the same, above-mentioned chief mechanisms ...'\". (3) Change the locator to \"checked against the page image\" and delete the \"Page 359 was read from the text layer only\" sentence. |\n| principle--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window | GO | Checked: the CAR quotes on PDF 359 (image) and PDF 360 (image), Letunov 1952, Prokor 1959 (the book's spelling), the re-adaptation definition at the top of PDF 360, Matveyev 1964, and Beckman 1961 and Yakovlev 1977. The Chapter 1 two-definitions point is confirmed in the vault `claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation`. Baseline line 75 matches (Explicit Q2, Q2a). `supported` is right. When NOT is substantive. | None required. Minor: the PDF 359 locator can now read \"checked against the page image\". The Bompa entry describes a *standard* load leading to a plateau, not a below-threshold one, so \"(below threshold, plateau ...)\" should read \"(an unchanging load, plateau; an excessive one, maladaptation)\". |\n| framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation | FIX | **Fig 6.23 values: all verified.** Panel A ends at about 144, 128, 114, 108 and 105%. A hump peaks at about 113% near week 8.8, then falls to the lowest end value; by end position and label order (maximum strength, absolute strength, speed) that is speed, and the entry's \"by that reading\" caveat is fair. Panel B lows run from about 83% to 96% at weeks 4-5. Curves cross back above 100% at about weeks 7-9 and end at about 157, 142-143, 129 (peak of about 130-131 near week 16.5), 120 and 114%. Dashed lines sit at weeks 6 and 12. Which curve dips deepest cannot be settled because the curves cross at weeks 7-9, as the entry says. **Problem 1:** When NOT has \"Figure against text: the figure's depressed phase (below 100% until about weeks 7-9) runs longer than the 3-4 week concentrated block\". This compares dip duration with block length, which are different quantities. The text on the same page says the concentrated volume sits \"in the first half of the training phase\" (PDF 360 image), and a dip through weeks 7-9 of 18 is that first half. Indicators are expected to stay low until volume drops. **Problem 2:** The Issurin (2016) critique is stated accurately: insufficient evidence for broad use in combat, team-sport and swimming preparation, with fitness gains but no reported effect on team practice or competition (p.6), and better suited to narrow target-quality sports (p.11), all as in the vault brief. But the entry does not say whose model is being criticised; per the brief, it is Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model, not Issurin's A-T-R. **Problem 3:** \"Not for novices or medium-level athletes, by the authors' own scope\". PDF 360 says the method \"was devised for highly qualified athletes\"; it does not forbid it for medium-level athletes. And 6.9.5 (`claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes`, PDF 371) says \"both methods are successful in training athletes of medium qualification\". Checked and correct: all the PDF 360-361 quotes, Komarova 1984 (\"he errs\"), and baseline line 105 (Inferred, \"Not claimed in the interview\"). `contested` stands, for the Issurin reason. | (1) Replace the Fig 6.23 \"figure against text\" sentence with: \"Panel B's depression lasts until about weeks 7-9 of 18, which fits the text's 'concentrated in the first half of the training phase' (PDF 360). The figure marks 6-week intervals (dashed lines at weeks 6 and 12) and shows no 3-4 week blocks or 7-10 day pauses, so it does not illustrate the block durations given on the same page. Its x-axis ends at 18 weeks; the text's period is 18-22 weeks.\" (2) Change \"Issurin (2016) judged the concentrated-unidirectional evidence ...\" to \"Issurin (2016) judged the evidence for Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model ...\". (3) Replace the last sentence with: \"The authors devised it for highly qualified athletes (PDF 360); in 6.9.5 they say both forms work at medium qualification (PDF 371), so it is not required there.\" Add the reciprocal link [[claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes]]. |\n| heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery | FIX | **`contested` is justified, but the conflict mixes up different quantities.** Sorted by what each number measures: (a) **Concentrated block or period length:** 3-4 weeks (PDF 360 / p.349, image); \"4-8 week concentrated blocks\" (PDF 305 / p.294); \"a limited period (of up to 2 months)\" (PDF 381 / p.370, image). These are the same quantity, so the conflict is real. (b) **Unidirectional training stage length:** \"unidirectional training over periods as long as 4-12 weeks\" (PDF 305) and \"prolonged stages (4-12 weeks)\" (PDF 381). This is a stage of one emphasis, not a concentrated block. (c) **Delayed-effect duration:** 4-12 weeks (PDF 375, recorded in the vault `heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next`). (d) **Latency to gains:** 1-3 weeks (PDF 305). (e) **Stage to exhaust the CAR:** 18-22 weeks (PDF 360). (f) **Upper limit for one primary emphasis:** months, by quality (staging 6.9.4 `claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality`, PDF 370-373). The entry files the 4-12 week figure under \"Contested inside the book (concentrated block duration)\", next to the 4-8 weeks, as if both were block lengths. Checked and correct: every numbered quote (PDF 360 image), Zhikharevich 1976 and Sirenko 1980, the arithmetic (17-21 and 11-15 weeks), and baseline line 183. The Issurin (six weeks) and Bondarchuk (four-week blocks) Related notes match the vault files. | Rewrite the When NOT conflict paragraph as: \"**Contested inside the book (concentrated block length).** 3-4 weeks here (PDF 360 / p.349); '4-8 week concentrated blocks' in 5.3.3 (PDF 305 / p.294); 'a limited period (of up to 2 months)' in 6.12.2 (PDF 381 / p.370). **Different quantities, not part of the conflict:** 4-12 weeks is the length of a unidirectional training *stage* (PDF 305, 381); 4-12 weeks is also how long the delayed training effect lasts (PDF 375, see [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]]); 1-3 weeks is the latency to gains (PDF 305); 18-22 weeks is the stage needed to exhaust the CAR (PDF 360).\" Keep the \"three 3-4 week blocks give about 9-12 weeks\" reconciliation, labelled Inference, as a link between block length (a) and stage length (b). Add as Inference: \"5.3.3 and 6.12.2 both define loading as concentrated by one month's volume (23-25% of the annual volume, PDF 305, 381), which makes the month the working unit; 'up to 2 months' reads as a ceiling.\" Add the link [[claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality]]. |\n| heuristic--restorative-stage-length-scales-with-homeostatic-disturbance | GO | Checked: the full restorative-stage quote against the PDF 361 / p.350 image, the \"after the volume of loading is decreased\" quote (PDF 360), the 1-3 week latency after 4-8 week blocks (PDF 305), and the 7-10 day pause (PDF 360). The Bompa & Haff quote matches the vault `allow-restoration` entry word for word. Baseline line 151 (Inferred) matches. The GPP mechanism is correctly marked as not given. The Example is labelled Inference. `supported` is right. | None. Minor: \"as quoted in the 5.3.3 entry above\" should read \"as quoted in [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]]\", since \"above\" means nothing in a standalone note. |\n| claim--bondarchuk-50-percent-more-volume-did-not-prolong-training-effect | FIX | (1) Process note in the entry body: \"The Table 6.1 and 6.2.2 material it points to is in batch 6a's pages (PDF pp. 334-336), not checked here.\" The brief forbids review or process history. (I confirmed that 6.2.2 starts on PDF 334 and Table 6.1 is referenced on PDF 335.) (2) Why it works opens \"Bondarchuk's study as reported does not test intensity at all; the comparison with intensity is the authors' reading\". That is a correct observation, but it is the writer's, presented without a label. Checked and correct: the full quote, including the italic \"the importance of paying careful attention to zones of loading intensity\" (PDF 361 image), and baseline line 105 (Inferred, \"Not claimed in the interview\"). The tension with the PDF 360 \"more extensive ... the longer the new functional level is maintained\" claim is accurate and labelled. `untested` is acceptable: one group, reported second-hand, with Bondarchuk's own \"strictly\" caveat. | (1) Replace the batch sentence with: \"The 6.2.2 intensity-zone material and Table 6.1 it points to are at PDF pp. 334-336 / printed pp. 323-325.\" (2) Prefix \"Inference:\" to the first sentence of Why it works. |\n| principle--functional-specialisation-is-a-primary-emphasis-not-an-exclusive-energy-system | FIX | (1) Why it works: \"For the cyclic side they point to Chapter 4's argument that endurance rests on muscular as well as respiratory qualities.\" PDF 362 / p.351 (image) points only to 2.2.3 and 5.4.3, and supports the cyclic point with Zhurbinoi (1978). There is no Chapter 4 pointer on the page. (2) \"(Zhurbinoi, 1978)\" is attached to \"strength preparedness, mainly in the form of strength-endurance, plays an important role\". In the source, the citation closes the *next* sentence (\"Research shows that, with a relatively moderate increase in muscular strength ... (Zhurbinoi, 1978)\"). Checked and correct: the 6.8.2 quotes (PDF 361-362 images), the \"classification scheme of 6.5\" note (a correct catch: the scheme is in 6.7), Sargent 1926, Henry 1951, Margaria 1963, and the triple jump example. Baseline line 123 (Inferred) and line 116 (Goals 1 and 2 are alactic and aerobic) match. | (1) Rewrite as: \"For the cyclic side the authors cite Zhurbinoi (1978) on strength-endurance (PDF 362). Inference: Chapter 4's argument that endurance rests on muscular as well as respiratory qualities (see Related) supports the same point.\" (2) Quote both sentences with the citation where the source puts it, or write: \"strength preparedness ... plays an important role\", followed by a study note (Zhurbinoi, 1978). |\n| claim--sprint-and-distance-training-emphasis-shifts-with-qualification-vankov | FIX | The title says \"Distance Runners Add Anaerobic Work at the Top\". The source (PDF 362, image) says \"distance athletes\", not runners. The entry body rightly calls the sentence \"Unclear as printed\", but the title settles that ambiguity as fact (\"Add\"). Checked and correct: the full Vankov quote, the class-level expansions, the swimmer comparison, the convergence link (labelled Inference), and baseline line 101 (Explicit Q1). `supported` is right. | Change the title to: \"Training Emphasis Shifts With Qualification: Sprinters Move From Aerobic Endurance and Speed to Speed Endurance; for Distance Athletes the Top-Level Emphasis Is Printed as Anaerobic (Vankov 1978)\". Keep the body's unclear-as-printed note as it is. |\n\nCounts: **GO 5, FIX 11, NO-GO 0.**\n\n## Special-attention findings\n\n- **Borrowed management theory.** All three entries name whose model it is: Yerkes and Dodson for the arousal curve, Vroom through Tellier (1978) for expectancy, and Hersey and Blanchard for situational leadership. None presents the general model as the authors' finding. The `untested` ratings are right, since the book gives no evidence for any of them. The one gap is the unsourced introvert/extrovert claim (arousal entry, FIX 2).\n- **Distributed versus concentrated `contested`.** The Issurin critique is stated accurately against the vault brief (p.6 and p.11) and is aimed at the concentrated-unidirectional model. The entry should say that model is Verkhoshansky's. The figure-against-text claim compares dip duration with block length, which are different quantities (FIX).\n- **Block-durations `contested`.** The rating holds: 3-4 weeks (PDF 360), 4-8 weeks (PDF 305) and \"up to 2 months\" (PDF 381) all measure concentrated block or period length. The 4-12 week figures measure something else (stage length at PDF 305 and 381, delayed-effect duration at PDF 375), and so does the 18-22 week figure (the stage needed to exhaust the CAR). The entry mixes these together (FIX).\n- **Fig 6.22.** Graph A decelerates; it is not straight. Two entries assert a contradiction with the text that does not exist, and claim a high-resolution crop check. This is the most serious error in the batch.\n- **Fig 6.23.** Every value the entries use is correct to within about 1 percentage point. The writer's panel A uncertainty is handled honestly. The panel B uncertainty about which curve dips deepest cannot be settled from the image.\n- **Baseline lines 116-117 (football energy systems).** Both are quoted accurately with the correct tags in `three-sport-groups`. Line 116 is quoted accurately in `functional-specialisation`. The source-against-baseline contrast on glycolytic share is presented fairly.\n\n## Missing (up to 5)\n\n1. **Decision-making and problem-solving (PDF 355-356 / p.344-345):** rate each problem high, medium or low on seriousness, urgency and growth; the six problem-solving steps; Maier's (1963) quality-versus-acceptance split and Roskin's four decision styles. This is the most directly usable part of 6.6 for running a department.\n2. **Management versus leadership (PDF 350, 353):** Tellier's management roles (responsibility, authority, accountability) set against leadership as influence (expertise, personality, charisma), and Oncken's four types of authority (position, personality, competence, integrity).\n3. **Blake-Mouton managerial grid and the Tannenbaum-Schmidt continuum with Likert's styles (PDF 353-355, Figs 6.17, 6.18 and 6.20):** the people-versus-production grid coordinates (9,1), (1,9), (1,1), (9,9) and (5,5). This could be one framework entry beside situational leadership.\n4. **Needs theories (PDF 352):** Maslow, Alderfer's ERG and McClelland's achievement, power and affiliation, with the authors' warning that Maslow's hierarchy \"has to be applied with circumspection\", and the transcendence and athletes note (Murphy & White, 1978).\n5. **The endurance monitoring limit (PDF 359):** \"The measures of aerobic and anaerobic output reflect the general level of special work capacity ... but they do not express the internal changes associated with adaptation.\" This is a monitoring point in its own right, and the compensatory entry mentions it only in passing.\n\n## Duplicates and reciprocal links\n\nThere are no duplicates. A grep of the vault and staging for Yerkes, Vroom, Hersey, Farfel, \"fundamental unit\", CAR, distributed loading, Bondarchuk 50% and Vankov found no entry that restates any of these 16. Suggested reciprocal links:\n\n- `framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading...` and staging 6.9.5 `claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes`.\n- `heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks...` and staging 6.9.4 `claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality`, plus vault `heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next` (for the PDF 375 delayed-effect duration).\n- `principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle...` and staging 6.2 `framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap`.\n- `principle--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window` and staging 6.9.4 `principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue`.\n\n## Overall verdict\n\n**GO AFTER FIXES.** Required fixes:\n\n1. Fig 6.22 misread in `four-observations` and `compensatory-adaptation`: graph A decelerates, so remove the figure-against-text claim.\n2. `distributed-vs-concentrated`: replace the Fig 6.23 dip-versus-block comparison; name Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model in the Issurin line; correct \"not for medium-level\".\n3. `concentrated-blocks`: sort the conflict by quantity (block 3-4 / 4-8 weeks / up to 2 months versus stage 4-12 weeks, delayed effect 4-12 weeks, CAR stage 18-22 weeks).\n4. Remove the batch-6a process notes from `mesocycle` and `bondarchuk`.\n5. Label the writer's reasoning as Inference: `mesocycle` Why it works, `functional-specialisation` Chapter 4 pointer, `bondarchuk` Why it works opener, `logical-scheme` \"only route\".\n6. Small source mismatches: `three-sport-groups` \"further subdivide\", `compensatory` \"taken much further\", `vankov` title, `arousal` curve B and the unsourced personality claim, `logical-scheme` shaded boxes, `functional-specialisation` Zhurbinoi placement.\n7. Change the PDF 359 locators from \"text layer only\" to \"checked against the page image\" (I have verified the page).\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"76359b7b-01d3-4e8b-b611-2a69d77a1ddb","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
